window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2023-06-2", "source": "2023年06月真题·第2套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose the student union of your university is organizing an online discussion on interpersonal relationships. You are to write an essay on ways to maintain a warm and friendly relationship with your classmates and on the benefits of such a relationship. You will have 30 minutes f or the task. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words .", "sample": "A warm and friendly relationship plays an important part in our campus life. Since we spend most of our time\nwith classmates, it is worth thinking about how to maintain such a relationship and what benefits it can bring.\nThere are several practical ways to keep such a relationship close. First, we should communicate sincerely. For\nexample, when a misunderstanding happens, we can simply talk it over honestly instead of staying cold.\nBesides, it also helps to support each other in study and daily life. When a classmate misses a lecture, sharing\nour notes and showing respect for different habits can quickly bring us closer.\nAs a result, such a relationship can bring us real benefits. It eases our pressure when courses get hard, pushes\nus to make progress together, and creates a strong sense of belonging in class. In short, building such warm\nbonds with our classmates is well worth our effort throughout the precious college years."}, "translation": {"prompt": "改革开放40多年以来，中国政府对高等教育越来越重视，高等教育已经进入稳步发展阶段。高校学生总数已接近4,700万人，位居世界第一。随着我国经济的快速发展，人民生活水平不断提高，越来越多的人渴望接受高等教育。我国高校的数量和学科专业持续增加，招生人数逐年上升，教学质量也在不断改进，为更多年轻人创造了接受高等教育的机会。", "sample": "Since the reform and opening-up over 40 years ago, the Chinese government has attached increasing importance to higher education, which has now entered a stage of steady development. Today, the total number of college students has reached nearly 47 million, ranking first in the world. With the rapid development of China's economy and the steady improvement of people's living standards, more and more people are eager to receive higher education. Meanwhile, the number of colleges and universities in China and the disciplines they offer continue to increase, student enrollment rises year by year, and teaching quality is constantly improving, creating opportunities for more young people to receive such education."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2023-06-2/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Morocco is responding to increasing energy demands by setting up one of the largest solar plants in the world.\nThe Noor solar power station is  __26__  in the city of Ouarzazate and, once completed, will generate 580 million watts of electricity. The World Bank estimates it will serve 1.1 million people. It's  __27__  to be completed soon.\nMorocco's current energy comes  __28__  from imports. The nation hopes to get 50 percent of its energy from renewable sources by 2030. With demand for energy  __29__  at an annual rate of 7 percent, the new solar plant could be a  __30__  part of that goal.\n\"This makes Morocco a big  __31__  in the field of solar energy in the Arab region and the African continent. It could also be a forerunner for many other countries in the world that  __32__  on foreign imports of energy,\" said Ali Hajji, a solar energy specialist and engineering professor.\nExperts believe that the Middle East and North Africa have huge  __33__  for solar energy projects. This is partly because of adequate sunlight and partly because technology has become more  __34__  in the region.\n\"The last few years have seen a realization of  __35__  how competitive solar technologies can be,\" said Michael Taylor, a senior analyst at the International Renewable Energy Agency.", "matchIntro": "New Formula One Chief Hopes to Grab Americans' Attention", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "For the past four decades, the leader of Formula One car racing, one of the biggest annual sporting series in the world, was Bernie Ecclestone, a former motorcycle parts dealer who built it into an international presence essentially on his own."}, {"label": "B", "text": "A skilled backroom operator who speaks without a filter, Ecclestone said often that in his opinion, the sport was at its best when he was allowed to act as \"a dictator.\""}, {"label": "C", "text": "Yet now the dictator is gone. After an American company, Liberty Media, acquired the Formula One competition recently, Chase Carey—a former executive with Fox Broadcasting Company and DirecTV who by his own admission is not a fierce racing fan—was named to replace Ecclestone and to try to renovate the organization's management, reach and ambition."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Among the goals, Carey said in an interview on Tuesday, is one that just about every global sport seems interested in chasing: increasing interest in the United States. \"People have said we're going to 'Americanize' it,\" Carey said. \"And we're not going to do that totally. But realistically, there are some elements of Americanization that the sport could use.\""}, {"label": "E", "text": "While Formula One commands enormous audiences throughout much of the world, many American sports fans know it as that other motorsport, the one that is not Nascar(纳斯卡车赛). Formula One teams race far more technologically advanced vehicles around tracks all over the world—in magnificent events in places like Malaysia, Monaco, Singapore and the United Arab Emirates, and on tradition-rich tracks like Silverstone in England and Monza in Italy too."}, {"label": "F", "text": "The series has an annual race in Austin, Texas. But within \"a few years,\" Carey said, he plans to bring another to a destination American city, like New York, Los Angeles, Miami or Las Vegas. Carey's ambitious plan is two-fold: first, change the business model of Formula One, which he said was a \"one-man show\" under Ecclestone that had a largely narrow vision when it came to negotiating partnership deals; and second, alter the way fans experience the sport, both in person and remotely, so that connections between the audience and people within the series are easier to make."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Increased digital access for fans, a more behind-the-scenes experience for broadcast viewers and innovation in areas like virtual reality—what is it like to speed around a track inside a Ferrari?—are among the possibilities. \"The sport has clearly been underserved,\" Carey said. \"It doesn't do anything digitally. There's no marketing. It doesn't tell any stories. The goal in this is to make the fans connect to the live experience as much as possible, and the tools you have to do that, we're not using at all.\""}, {"label": "H", "text": "The larger question, though, is a familiar one: Is there room for Formula One in the ever-crowded sports landscape of the United States? Opinions vary, particularly because viewing habits among consumers continue to evolve. John Bloom, a professor at Shippensburg University who has studied American sports history, said the biggest challenge for any sport trying to increase its presence in the United States was framing itself in a way that had lasting appeal. \"Sports generally become popular in some way because they establish a narrative,\" Bloom said. \"When I think of motorsports in the U.S., what we all think of is Nascar, and the narrative of Nascar is sort of rural, white, working-class Americans, mostly in the South, connecting with the atmosphere of those races. That's the narrative. When I think of the narrative of Formula One, it's a very different kind of audience.\""}, {"label": "I", "text": "That difference, Carey said, is significant. While some might immediately link Formula One to Nascar in terms of American growth, Carey said Formula One's brand research had indicated there was very little crossover; rather, Formula One fans generally cite other so-called elite events, like Wimbledon or the Ryder Cup, as competitions they enjoy. \"Other than they're both cars, the Nascar fan base is a very different fan base,\" Carey said. \"It's a very regional fan base. Formula One is a global, famous brand of stars. These are machines that shock and awe you.\""}, {"label": "J", "text": "Carey's background is in deal making and innovation. At Fox Broadcasting Company, he was a top advisor for years, known for his skill in helping to lead the launch of the company into sports, as well as the start of Fox News Channel. After going to DirecTV, he positioned the satellite provider as a mainstream option in millions of households."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Now, after Liberty Media paid $4.4 billion to acquire Formula One, he is charged with making the investment pay off. \"I think they can build Formula One in the U.S.,\" said Patrick Crakes, an executive at InVivo Media Group who spent 25 years at Fox Broadcasting Company before leaving in 2016 as a senior manager at Fox Sports. \"People don't work on their cars anymore. They don't want that connection anymore. It's about technology and pushing the limits. It's about speed, danger and risk. And Formula One has that more than any other racing series.\""}, {"label": "L", "text": "That is what hooked Carey, and he said he thought his experience was not unusual. He recalled attending Formula One's Monaco race last year and being overwhelmed by the ceremony leading up to the event, the way the race charmed the city for days ahead of the start. In his mind, it felt like a Super Bowl(超级碗橄榄球赛)."}, {"label": "M", "text": "Then, on race day, he watched as the cars rocketed out of a tunnel and went screaming toward a tight turn with the city's harbor and the Mediterranean Sea in the background framing the scene. He was fascinated. \"You can't help but be awed,\" he said, \"and I think that feeling can be translated to the viewer.\""}, {"label": "N", "text": "He added: \"The broader sport is a little too inward-looking, and we need to be more open. In some ways, I'm glad to be coming from the outside. The guys who are in the sport forever are sitting there saying: 'We can't do that. We can't do that because it's never been done that way.'\""}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Supermarkets have long been suffering as one of the thinnest-margined businesses in existence and one of the least-looked-forward-to places to work or visit. For more than a decade, they have been under attack from e-commerce giants, blamed for making Americans fat, and accused of contributing to climate change.\nSupermarkets can technically be defined as giants housing 15,000 to 60,000 different products. The revolutionary idea of a self-service grocery, where people could hunt and gather food from aisles rather than asking a clerk to fetch items from behind a counter, first came about in America. There is some debate about which was the very first, but over the years a consensus has built around King Kullen Supermarket, founded in New York in 1930.\nFor some 300 years, Americans had fed themselves from small stores and public markets. Shopping for food involved mud, noisy chickens, clouds of flies, nasty smells, bargaining, and getting short-changed. The supermarket imitated the Fordist factory, with its emphasis on efficiency and standardization, and reimagined it as a place to buy food. Supermarkets may not feel cutting-edge now, but they were a revolution in distribution at the time. They were such strange marvels that, on her first official state visit to the United States in 1957, Queen Elizabeth II insisted on an impromptu(即兴的) tour of a suburban-Maryland Giant Food.\nThe typical supermarket layout has barely changed over the past 90 years. Most stores open with flowers, fruit and vegetables at the front as a breath of freshness to arouse our appetite. Meanwhile, they keep the milk, eggs, and other daily basics all the way back so you'll travel through as much of the store as possible, and be tempted along the way.\nIn the early days, as the supermarket multiplied, so did our suspicion of it. We have long feared that this \"revolution in distribution\" uses corporate black magic on our appetite. The book The Hidden Persuaders, published in 1957, warned that supermarkets were putting women in a \"hypnoidal trance(催眠恍惚状态),\" causing them to wander aisles bumping into boxes and \"picking things off shelves at random.\"", "47": "Supermarkets have long been suffering as one of the thinnest-margined businesses in existence and one of the least-looked-forward-to places to work or visit. For more than a decade, they have been under attack from e-commerce giants, blamed for making Americans fat, and accused of contributing to climate change.\nSupermarkets can technically be defined as giants housing 15,000 to 60,000 different products. The revolutionary idea of a self-service grocery, where people could hunt and gather food from aisles rather than asking a clerk to fetch items from behind a counter, first came about in America. There is some debate about which was the very first, but over the years a consensus has built around King Kullen Supermarket, founded in New York in 1930.\nFor some 300 years, Americans had fed themselves from small stores and public markets. Shopping for food involved mud, noisy chickens, clouds of flies, nasty smells, bargaining, and getting short-changed. The supermarket imitated the Fordist factory, with its emphasis on efficiency and standardization, and reimagined it as a place to buy food. Supermarkets may not feel cutting-edge now, but they were a revolution in distribution at the time. They were such strange marvels that, on her first official state visit to the United States in 1957, Queen Elizabeth II insisted on an impromptu(即兴的) tour of a suburban-Maryland Giant Food.\nThe typical supermarket layout has barely changed over the past 90 years. Most stores open with flowers, fruit and vegetables at the front as a breath of freshness to arouse our appetite. Meanwhile, they keep the milk, eggs, and other daily basics all the way back so you'll travel through as much of the store as possible, and be tempted along the way.\nIn the early days, as the supermarket multiplied, so did our suspicion of it. We have long feared that this \"revolution in distribution\" uses corporate black magic on our appetite. The book The Hidden Persuaders, published in 1957, warned that supermarkets were putting women in a \"hypnoidal trance(催眠恍惚状态),\" causing them to wander aisles bumping into boxes and \"picking things off shelves at random.\"", "48": "Supermarkets have long been suffering as one of the thinnest-margined businesses in existence and one of the least-looked-forward-to places to work or visit. For more than a decade, they have been under attack from e-commerce giants, blamed for making Americans fat, and accused of contributing to climate change.\nSupermarkets can technically be defined as giants housing 15,000 to 60,000 different products. The revolutionary idea of a self-service grocery, where people could hunt and gather food from aisles rather than asking a clerk to fetch items from behind a counter, first came about in America. There is some debate about which was the very first, but over the years a consensus has built around King Kullen Supermarket, founded in New York in 1930.\nFor some 300 years, Americans had fed themselves from small stores and public markets. Shopping for food involved mud, noisy chickens, clouds of flies, nasty smells, bargaining, and getting short-changed. The supermarket imitated the Fordist factory, with its emphasis on efficiency and standardization, and reimagined it as a place to buy food. Supermarkets may not feel cutting-edge now, but they were a revolution in distribution at the time. They were such strange marvels that, on her first official state visit to the United States in 1957, Queen Elizabeth II insisted on an impromptu(即兴的) tour of a suburban-Maryland Giant Food.\nThe typical supermarket layout has barely changed over the past 90 years. Most stores open with flowers, fruit and vegetables at the front as a breath of freshness to arouse our appetite. Meanwhile, they keep the milk, eggs, and other daily basics all the way back so you'll travel through as much of the store as possible, and be tempted along the way.\nIn the early days, as the supermarket multiplied, so did our suspicion of it. We have long feared that this \"revolution in distribution\" uses corporate black magic on our appetite. The book The Hidden Persuaders, published in 1957, warned that supermarkets were putting women in a \"hypnoidal trance(催眠恍惚状态),\" causing them to wander aisles bumping into boxes and \"picking things off shelves at random.\"", "49": "Supermarkets have long been suffering as one of the thinnest-margined businesses in existence and one of the least-looked-forward-to places to work or visit. For more than a decade, they have been under attack from e-commerce giants, blamed for making Americans fat, and accused of contributing to climate change.\nSupermarkets can technically be defined as giants housing 15,000 to 60,000 different products. The revolutionary idea of a self-service grocery, where people could hunt and gather food from aisles rather than asking a clerk to fetch items from behind a counter, first came about in America. There is some debate about which was the very first, but over the years a consensus has built around King Kullen Supermarket, founded in New York in 1930.\nFor some 300 years, Americans had fed themselves from small stores and public markets. Shopping for food involved mud, noisy chickens, clouds of flies, nasty smells, bargaining, and getting short-changed. The supermarket imitated the Fordist factory, with its emphasis on efficiency and standardization, and reimagined it as a place to buy food. Supermarkets may not feel cutting-edge now, but they were a revolution in distribution at the time. They were such strange marvels that, on her first official state visit to the United States in 1957, Queen Elizabeth II insisted on an impromptu(即兴的) tour of a suburban-Maryland Giant Food.\nThe typical supermarket layout has barely changed over the past 90 years. Most stores open with flowers, fruit and vegetables at the front as a breath of freshness to arouse our appetite. Meanwhile, they keep the milk, eggs, and other daily basics all the way back so you'll travel through as much of the store as possible, and be tempted along the way.\nIn the early days, as the supermarket multiplied, so did our suspicion of it. We have long feared that this \"revolution in distribution\" uses corporate black magic on our appetite. The book The Hidden Persuaders, published in 1957, warned that supermarkets were putting women in a \"hypnoidal trance(催眠恍惚状态),\" causing them to wander aisles bumping into boxes and \"picking things off shelves at random.\"", "50": "Supermarkets have long been suffering as one of the thinnest-margined businesses in existence and one of the least-looked-forward-to places to work or visit. For more than a decade, they have been under attack from e-commerce giants, blamed for making Americans fat, and accused of contributing to climate change.\nSupermarkets can technically be defined as giants housing 15,000 to 60,000 different products. The revolutionary idea of a self-service grocery, where people could hunt and gather food from aisles rather than asking a clerk to fetch items from behind a counter, first came about in America. There is some debate about which was the very first, but over the years a consensus has built around King Kullen Supermarket, founded in New York in 1930.\nFor some 300 years, Americans had fed themselves from small stores and public markets. Shopping for food involved mud, noisy chickens, clouds of flies, nasty smells, bargaining, and getting short-changed. The supermarket imitated the Fordist factory, with its emphasis on efficiency and standardization, and reimagined it as a place to buy food. Supermarkets may not feel cutting-edge now, but they were a revolution in distribution at the time. They were such strange marvels that, on her first official state visit to the United States in 1957, Queen Elizabeth II insisted on an impromptu(即兴的) tour of a suburban-Maryland Giant Food.\nThe typical supermarket layout has barely changed over the past 90 years. Most stores open with flowers, fruit and vegetables at the front as a breath of freshness to arouse our appetite. Meanwhile, they keep the milk, eggs, and other daily basics all the way back so you'll travel through as much of the store as possible, and be tempted along the way.\nIn the early days, as the supermarket multiplied, so did our suspicion of it. We have long feared that this \"revolution in distribution\" uses corporate black magic on our appetite. The book The Hidden Persuaders, published in 1957, warned that supermarkets were putting women in a \"hypnoidal trance(催眠恍惚状态),\" causing them to wander aisles bumping into boxes and \"picking things off shelves at random.\"", "51": "The traditional school year, with three months of vacation every summer, was first implemented when America was an agricultural society and the summer months were needed for farm work. Since then, we've completely changed as a nation. Students no longer spend summers farming, but they aren't in school, either. The average American student receives 13 weeks off from school each calendar year-with about 11 of those during the summer. Few other countries have more than seven weeks off in a school calendar.\nWith the U.S. lagging behind other countries in academics, it's time to consider year-round schooling. One benefit of this change is that students will not fall victim to the \"summer slide,\" or the well-documented phenomenon where students forget some of the knowledge they have acquired when too much time is taken off from school. Decades of research shows that it can take from 8 to 13 weeks at the beginning of every school year for students to get back to where they were before the summer holiday.\nBut year-round schooling isn't just about academics. Teachers and students experience a closer relationship in year-round schools than they do in traditional schools and, in the absence of any long-term break, students do not feel detached from the school environment. These closer bonds and greater attachment pay off. Research shows that students in year-round schools are more self-confident and feel more positive about their schooling experience.\nBut don't kids need time to relax? Some childhood development experts believe that time off from school is vital to healthy development as kids are not designed to spend so much of their time inside classrooms and the summer break provides a perfect opportunity to get outside. The problem with this argument is that most children aren't playing outside or even spending time with other kids. While some children visit summer camps, most stay at home, watching TV or playing games on electronic devices, which hardly benefits them.\nThe U.S. has changed from a farming economy to a knowledge- and innovation-based economy, so it makes sense for the school year to change as well.", "52": "The traditional school year, with three months of vacation every summer, was first implemented when America was an agricultural society and the summer months were needed for farm work. Since then, we've completely changed as a nation. Students no longer spend summers farming, but they aren't in school, either. The average American student receives 13 weeks off from school each calendar year-with about 11 of those during the summer. Few other countries have more than seven weeks off in a school calendar.\nWith the U.S. lagging behind other countries in academics, it's time to consider year-round schooling. One benefit of this change is that students will not fall victim to the \"summer slide,\" or the well-documented phenomenon where students forget some of the knowledge they have acquired when too much time is taken off from school. Decades of research shows that it can take from 8 to 13 weeks at the beginning of every school year for students to get back to where they were before the summer holiday.\nBut year-round schooling isn't just about academics. Teachers and students experience a closer relationship in year-round schools than they do in traditional schools and, in the absence of any long-term break, students do not feel detached from the school environment. These closer bonds and greater attachment pay off. Research shows that students in year-round schools are more self-confident and feel more positive about their schooling experience.\nBut don't kids need time to relax? Some childhood development experts believe that time off from school is vital to healthy development as kids are not designed to spend so much of their time inside classrooms and the summer break provides a perfect opportunity to get outside. The problem with this argument is that most children aren't playing outside or even spending time with other kids. While some children visit summer camps, most stay at home, watching TV or playing games on electronic devices, which hardly benefits them.\nThe U.S. has changed from a farming economy to a knowledge- and innovation-based economy, so it makes sense for the school year to change as well.", "53": "The traditional school year, with three months of vacation every summer, was first implemented when America was an agricultural society and the summer months were needed for farm work. Since then, we've completely changed as a nation. Students no longer spend summers farming, but they aren't in school, either. The average American student receives 13 weeks off from school each calendar year-with about 11 of those during the summer. Few other countries have more than seven weeks off in a school calendar.\nWith the U.S. lagging behind other countries in academics, it's time to consider year-round schooling. One benefit of this change is that students will not fall victim to the \"summer slide,\" or the well-documented phenomenon where students forget some of the knowledge they have acquired when too much time is taken off from school. Decades of research shows that it can take from 8 to 13 weeks at the beginning of every school year for students to get back to where they were before the summer holiday.\nBut year-round schooling isn't just about academics. Teachers and students experience a closer relationship in year-round schools than they do in traditional schools and, in the absence of any long-term break, students do not feel detached from the school environment. These closer bonds and greater attachment pay off. Research shows that students in year-round schools are more self-confident and feel more positive about their schooling experience.\nBut don't kids need time to relax? Some childhood development experts believe that time off from school is vital to healthy development as kids are not designed to spend so much of their time inside classrooms and the summer break provides a perfect opportunity to get outside. The problem with this argument is that most children aren't playing outside or even spending time with other kids. While some children visit summer camps, most stay at home, watching TV or playing games on electronic devices, which hardly benefits them.\nThe U.S. has changed from a farming economy to a knowledge- and innovation-based economy, so it makes sense for the school year to change as well.", "54": "The traditional school year, with three months of vacation every summer, was first implemented when America was an agricultural society and the summer months were needed for farm work. Since then, we've completely changed as a nation. Students no longer spend summers farming, but they aren't in school, either. The average American student receives 13 weeks off from school each calendar year-with about 11 of those during the summer. Few other countries have more than seven weeks off in a school calendar.\nWith the U.S. lagging behind other countries in academics, it's time to consider year-round schooling. One benefit of this change is that students will not fall victim to the \"summer slide,\" or the well-documented phenomenon where students forget some of the knowledge they have acquired when too much time is taken off from school. Decades of research shows that it can take from 8 to 13 weeks at the beginning of every school year for students to get back to where they were before the summer holiday.\nBut year-round schooling isn't just about academics. Teachers and students experience a closer relationship in year-round schools than they do in traditional schools and, in the absence of any long-term break, students do not feel detached from the school environment. These closer bonds and greater attachment pay off. Research shows that students in year-round schools are more self-confident and feel more positive about their schooling experience.\nBut don't kids need time to relax? Some childhood development experts believe that time off from school is vital to healthy development as kids are not designed to spend so much of their time inside classrooms and the summer break provides a perfect opportunity to get outside. The problem with this argument is that most children aren't playing outside or even spending time with other kids. While some children visit summer camps, most stay at home, watching TV or playing games on electronic devices, which hardly benefits them.\nThe U.S. has changed from a farming economy to a knowledge- and innovation-based economy, so it makes sense for the school year to change as well.", "55": "The traditional school year, with three months of vacation every summer, was first implemented when America was an agricultural society and the summer months were needed for farm work. Since then, we've completely changed as a nation. Students no longer spend summers farming, but they aren't in school, either. The average American student receives 13 weeks off from school each calendar year-with about 11 of those during the summer. Few other countries have more than seven weeks off in a school calendar.\nWith the U.S. lagging behind other countries in academics, it's time to consider year-round schooling. One benefit of this change is that students will not fall victim to the \"summer slide,\" or the well-documented phenomenon where students forget some of the knowledge they have acquired when too much time is taken off from school. Decades of research shows that it can take from 8 to 13 weeks at the beginning of every school year for students to get back to where they were before the summer holiday.\nBut year-round schooling isn't just about academics. Teachers and students experience a closer relationship in year-round schools than they do in traditional schools and, in the absence of any long-term break, students do not feel detached from the school environment. These closer bonds and greater attachment pay off. Research shows that students in year-round schools are more self-confident and feel more positive about their schooling experience.\nBut don't kids need time to relax? Some childhood development experts believe that time off from school is vital to healthy development as kids are not designed to spend so much of their time inside classrooms and the summer break provides a perfect opportunity to get outside. The problem with this argument is that most children aren't playing outside or even spending time with other kids. While some children visit summer camps, most stay at home, watching TV or playing games on electronic devices, which hardly benefits them.\nThe U.S. has changed from a farming economy to a knowledge- and innovation-based economy, so it makes sense for the school year to change as well."}, "questions": [{"id": "2023-06-2-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What do we learn from the police report?", "options": ["A) A man was bitten by a snake.", "B) A man was taken to a hospital.", "C) A man kept a 4-foot snake as a pet.", "D) A man fell off his toilet seat."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） A man was bitten by a snake.\n【定位】Police have reported a man being bitten by a 4-foot snake when he lifted his toilet seat on\nSunday morning.\n警方报告称，周日早晨一名男子在掀开马桶盖时被一条 4 英尺长的蛇咬伤。\n【信号】新闻导语句即答案。答案句就是 Police have reported a man being bitten by a 4-foot\nsnake when he lifted his toilet seat on Sunday morning. 这一句——新闻报道的第一句\n（导语）就是 Police have reported…，是\"警方报告\"信号的直接落点。新闻题里只要题干\n问\"警方/官方/这则报道说什么\"，几乎都落在导语句。\n【替换】a man being bitten by a 4-foot snake ↔ A man was bitten by a snake.\n非谓语转过去时·同义直接对应\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n录音第二段明确说 the man did not have to go to the hospital——这位男子**没有**去\n医院，只是在现场处理了手臂。选项 A man was taken to a hospital. 盗用了原文的\nhospital 一词却把否定砍掉，反成肯定，是典型\"听到 hospital 就想选\"的陷阱。\n借用原文：the man did not have to go to the hospital\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 A man kept a 4-foot snake as a pet. 把录音第 1 段里的 a 4-foot snake 原词搬过\n来，看似耳熟。但录音第 3 段明确说 the snake didn't belong to the man，蛇并不是男子\n的，更谈不上 kept…as a pet。这是\"听到原词就想选\"的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：a 4-foot snake\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 A man fell off his toilet seat. 借用了录音 he lifted his toilet seat 中的 toilet seat\n这块\"熟面孔\"，但把动作偷换成了 fell off（从马桶上摔下来）。录音说的是\"他掀开马桶\n盖\"，根本没说他摔倒。这又是一处原词陷阱叠加偷换概念。\n借用原文：he lifted his toilet seat", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What does the report say was still unclear?", "options": ["A) Where the snake had been taken.", "B) Whether the snake was infected.", "C) How the snake was captured.", "D) Who owned the snake."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Who owned the snake.\n【定位】It was unclear who the owner was. The snake may be taken to a local zoo for care if the\nowner does not come forward.\n蛇的主人是谁尚不清楚。如果主人不出面认领，这条蛇可能会被送到当地一家动物园照料。\n【信号】unclear + who 直接锁定。答案信号是 It was unclear who the owner was. 中的 unclear\n一词——它与题干 still unclear 直接对应；unclear 后面跟的 who the owner was 就是\"未\n明的内容\"，几乎是把答案塞到题干同款词的旁边。\n【替换】It was unclear who the owner was. ↔ Who owned the snake.\n名词转动词·who 从句同义重写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音 The snake was eventually captured by the local animal handlers and taken to\nan animal hospital afterward. 已经明确告诉我们蛇被送去了 animal hospital；之后还补\n了一句若无主认领\"可能送到当地动物园\"，去向并非\"unclear\"。选项 Where the snake\nhad been taken. 借用 taken 这一原词制造\"哪里去了仍不清楚\"的错觉，实际录音对去向已\n交代。\n借用原文：taken to an animal hospital afterward\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音 It was being treated for an outer skin infection. 明确说蛇正在因外皮感染接受治\n疗，感染状况已确认而非未明。选项 Whether the snake was infected. 把 infection 一词\n盗作诱饵，骗\"听到 infection 就想选\"的考生。\n借用原文：It was being treated for an outer skin infection.\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音 The snake was eventually captured by the local animal handlers 明确说蛇是被当\n地的\"动物管理员\"捕获的，捕获方式（由谁、怎么抓）已交代。选项 How the snake was\ncaptured. 直接挪用 captured 一词，把已说清的事伪装成 unclear，是经典原词陷阱。\n借用原文：The snake was eventually captured by the local animal handlers\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 Every week since last April, Andrea Belcher has been doing something slightly unusual and amusing\nwhen she takes out her trash.\n自去年四月以来,每周当Andrea Belcher倒垃圾时,她都会做一件略显不寻常而有趣的事。\n2 It all started off when a movie star shared a photo of herself putting her trash bins out in a ball\ngown. This inspired Andrea to get dressed up to take out the trash.\n这一切始于一位电影明星分享了一张自己穿着晚礼服倒垃圾的照片。这启发了Andrea盛装打扮去倒\n垃圾。\n3 She was too embarrassed to do it during the day. So one dark evening, she put on her black off-the-\nshoulder gown and put her trash bin out. Her daughter filmed it and the whole family laughed, but it\nseemed to go down well.\n她白天不好意思这样做。所以在一个漆黑的夜晚,她穿上了黑色露肩礼服去倒垃圾桶。她女儿拍摄了这一\n幕,全家人都笑了,但效果似乎不错。\n4 The following weeks, she found other outfits in her dressing-up box, and dressed up as different\ncharacters and famous people, sharing her exploits on social media. Before long, people started offering\nup their own fancy dresses for her to wear. And before she knew it, she had done 20 weeks.\n接下来的几周,她从自己的装扮箱里找出其他服装,打扮成不同的角色和名人,在社交媒体上分享她的趣事。\n不久之后,人们开始主动提供自己的华丽服装给她穿。在她意识到之前,她已经坚持了20周。\n5 Explaining why she has kept up the videos, Andrea explained how nice it was to have a little bit of\nfun and craziness that made people smile. Besides, it also helps to remind people in the community to\nput out their trash bins each week.\n在解释为什么她一直坚持拍摄这些视频时,Andrea说能有一点乐趣和疯狂让人们微笑是多么美好。此外,这\n也有助于提醒社区里的人们每周倒垃圾桶。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What has Andrea Belcher been doing since last April?", "options": ["A) Taking her trash out in fancy dresses.", "B) Amusing herself by going to ball parties.", "C) Sharing her photos with famous movie stars.", "D) Posting her daughter's photos on social media."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Taking her trash out in fancy dresses.\n【定位】Every week since last April, Andrea Belcher has been doing something slightly unusual and\namusing when she takes out her trash.\n自去年四月起，安德烈娅·贝尔彻每周倒垃圾的时候都会做一件稍微有点不寻常又搞笑的事。\n【信号】开篇点题句 + 时间状语锁定。新闻开篇 Every week since last April 与题干 since last\nApril 完全对应，时间状语就是答案信号；后半句 when she takes out her trash 直接揭示\n行为载体——倒垃圾，这是承载答案的那句话。\n【替换】doing something slightly unusual and amusing when she takes out her trash ↔\nTaking her trash out in fancy dresses\n概括 + 后文具体细化\nget dressed up to take out the trash ↔ in fancy dresses\nget dressed up = 穿正装 = fancy dresses\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音第2段提到 a movie star shared a photo of herself putting her trash bins out in a\nball gown，ball gown（晚礼服）只是她穿的衣服样式，并不是说\"她去参加舞会\"。选项 B\n把 ball 偷换成 ball parties，把\"穿礼服倒垃圾\"硬扭成\"去舞会自娱\"，是典型的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：a movie star shared a photo of herself putting her trash bins out in a ball gown\nC) 张冠李戴\n录音第2段明确 a movie star shared a photo of herself putting her trash bins out in a\nball gown——分享照片的人是那位明星，分享的内容是明星自己的照片。选项 C 把动作主\n语换成了 Andrea，把\"她和明星分享照片\"安到她头上，是张冠李戴。\n借用原文：a movie star shared a photo of herself putting her trash bins out in a ball gown\nD) 张冠李戴\n录音第3段说 Her daughter filmed it，是女儿帮她拍视频；第4段说 sharing her exploits\non social media，分享的是\"她自己的事迹\"。选项 D 却说\"发布她女儿的照片\"，把\"女儿帮\n拍\"偷换成\"发布女儿的照片\"，主客颠倒。\n借用原文：Her daughter filmed it", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "Why has Andrea Belcher kept up her videos?", "options": ["A) To make herself popular.", "B) To amuse people.", "C) To please her daughter.", "D) To record her achievements."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） To amuse people.\n【定位】Explaining why she has kept up the videos, Andrea explained how nice it was to have a little\nbit of fun and craziness that made people smile.\n在解释为什么坚持拍这些视频时，安德烈娅说，能有一点点乐趣和疯狂，让人们露出笑容，\n这感觉真好。\n【信号】结尾解释句直接对应题干。录音第5段开头 Explaining why she has kept up the videos\n与题干 Why has Andrea Belcher kept up her videos? 是几乎逐字对应的解释信号；其后\n的 a little bit of fun and craziness that made people smile 就是她给出的真实原因，是\n承载答案的那句话。\n【替换】a little bit of fun and craziness that made people smile ↔ To amuse people\nmade people smile = amuse people\nfun and craziness ↔ amuse\nfun ≈ amuse 的同根义\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音里完全没有出现 popular/famous 类的动机描述；她坚持的原因被明确说成 to have a\nlittle bit of fun and craziness that made people smile，与\"让自己出名\"无关。选项 A\n是\"花絮新闻最常见的诱选动机\"——预读时已警惕。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音第3段有 Her daughter filmed it and the whole family laughed，daughter 真的被\n念到，但只是说女儿帮她拍、全家笑——这不是她坚持的目的。选项 C 把 daughter 偷过\n来，硬安成\"为了讨女儿欢心\"，是典型原词陷阱。\n借用原文：Her daughter filmed it and the whole family laughed\nD) 未提及\n录音里既没有 record（记录）也没有 achievements（成就）类的表达；she had done 20\nweeks 只是叙述事实，并非\"为了记录成就\"。选项 D 是凭\"长期坚持\"脑补出来的，与解释信\n号后给出的真实动机不符。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What can people do at Frank's restaurant?", "options": ["A) Eat as much as they want for $10.", "B) Have a chance of winning a $100 prize.", "C) Have a meal even if they have no money.", "D) Get a free meal after answering some questions."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Have a meal even if they have no money.\n【定位】No money but still hungry? No problem! Welcome to Frank's restaurant where customers are\npaying for meals and leaving receipts on a board. Anyone who is hungry and wants\nsomething to eat can take one, no questions asked and hand it to the staff for some food.\n没钱但还是饿着？没关系！欢迎来到 Frank's 餐厅——这里的顾客先付饭钱，再把小票留在\n一块板上。任何饿了想吃东西的人都可以取下一张小票，无需任何询问，把它交给店员就能\n换到一份食物。\n【信号】导语句直接点题。开头连用两个设问 No money but still hungry? No problem!，紧接着\n用 Welcome to… 引出餐厅做法，这是新闻典型的导语式信号——把整则新闻的核心事实押\n在第一段第一句。听到设问+欢迎句出现，就要在心里把笔停在这一句。\n【替换】No money but still hungry? No problem! ↔ even if they have no money\n设问反问 = 让步条件\nAnyone who is hungry and wants something to eat can take one, no questions asked and hand it to\nthe staff for some food\n↔ Have a meal\nhand it to the staff for some food = Have a meal\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音第三段确实提到每份饭 each costing about $10，但说的是每张小票背后对应的餐\n费，并非顾客只要付 $10 就能 Eat as much as they want；选项盗用 $10 这个原词，把它\n扭成自助式的随便吃，是典型的听到数字就想选式陷阱。\n借用原文：each costing about $10\nB)\n录音第二段说 a donor came in with $100 for each member of its staff，$100 是某位捐\n赠者发给员工的人均款项，根本不是 a $100 prize 抽奖奖金；选项盗用 $100 这个原词，\n再嫁接 prize 制造抽奖联想。\n借用原文：a donor came in with $100 for each member of its staff\nD)\n录音里 no questions asked 明确说取餐时不需要回答任何问题，而 Get a free meal after\nanswering some questions 偏偏要求顾客 answering some questions 才能得到免费餐，\n与原文方向相反；选项盗用 questions 这个原词，却反转了它的语义。\n借用原文：no questions asked", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the owner of the restaurant say about the idea for the board of free meals?", "options": ["A) It was brought up by two staffers.", "B) It helped to popularize her restaurant.", "C) It originated from a donation to her staff.", "D) It was suggested by some of her customers."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It originated from a donation to her staff.\n【定位】The idea for the board of free meals started after a donor came in with $100 for each member\nof its staff. After two staffers returned to their share, there was $700 leftover according to\nTarah Morris, the owner of the restaurant, so they decided to do something more by creating\nthe board.\n餐厅老板 Tarah Morris 说，这块免费餐板的点子始于一位捐赠者走进店里、给每位员工\n100 美元；其中两名员工把自己那份退了回去，于是剩下了 700 美元，他们便决定用这笔\n钱做点更有意义的事，就这样有了这块板。\n【信号】according to + owner 的转述信号。录音里 according to Tarah Morris, the owner of\nthe restaurant 是典型的新闻转述信号，把整段缘起绑定到店主之口，正好对应题干 the\nowner…say about。听到 according to + 人物身份，就要在心里给整段打上本人原话的\n标签。\n【替换】The idea for the board of free meals started after a donor came in with $100 for each member of its\nstaff\n↔ originated from a donation\nstarted after = originated from\n$100 for each member of its staff ↔ to her staff\nfor each member of its staff = to her staff\n【排除】A)\n录音里 After two staffers returned to their share 说的是两名员工把分到的 $100 退了回\n来，他们的动作是 returned，而不是 brought up the idea；选项盗用 two staffers 这个\n原词，把退款人偷换为想法的提出者，是张冠李戴。\n借用原文：After two staffers returned to their share\nB) 未提及\n店主谈的是 idea 怎么开始的，整段录音从未提及这块板让餐厅本身变得更出名或更受欢\n迎，popularize her restaurant 在原文中找不到对应信息，是用主题词撑起的无中生有\n项。\nD)\n录音里把 idea 的发起归到店主与员工的商量（they decided to do something more by\ncreating the board），并未出现顾客建议的桥段；suggested by some of her\ncustomers 把提议人替换成 customers，与原文角色不符，是典型的张冠李戴。\n借用原文：they decided to do something more by creating the board", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What has happened at Frank's restaurant since the board of free meals was created?", "options": ["A) Fifty customers have offered donations.", "B) More people have been giving than taking.", "C) Many people have come to eat at the restaurant.", "D) Most staffers have received messages of kindness."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） More people have been giving than taking.\n【定位】The drive has been going on for two weeks and Morris said just under 50 customers have\npicked up a free meal. There have been no reports of anyone taking advantage of the\nsituation. In fact, there are a lot more people giving tickets than receiving.\n这场公益行动已经持续了两周，Morris 说接近 50 名顾客取走了免费餐；尚未出现任何人趁\n机占便宜的情况；事实上，捐出小票的人远多于来取走小票的人。\n【信号】In fact 强调真正结论。录音用 In fact, there are a lot more people giving tickets than\nreceiving，In fact 是新闻里典型的强调真正结论的信号词，把前文铺垫与轻描淡写一笔翻\n起来，落到本则新闻的核心收束。听到 In fact 就要在心里抢一拍：这后面是答案落点。\n【替换】there are a lot more people giving tickets than receiving ↔\nMore people have been giving than taking\nmore…than 结构原样保留\ngiving tickets ↔ giving\n省略宾语而义不变\n【排除】A)\n录音里 just under 50 customers have picked up a free meal，50 这个数字指的是取走\n免费餐的顾客接近 50 人，而不是 Fifty customers have offered donations 提供捐款的\n人；选项盗用 50 这个原词数字，把动作从 picked up 偷换为 offered donations，是经典\n的数字原词陷阱。\n借用原文：just under 50 customers have picked up a free meal\nC)\nMany people have come to eat at the restaurant 只说很多人来吃饭，是泛化的常识陈\n述；录音真正强调的是 giving 与 taking 的对比，而不是来店人数。选项盗用 people、\neat、restaurant 这类高频主题词，制造听起来都对的错觉。\n借用原文：just under 50 customers have picked up a free meal\nD)\n录音里 The board has messages of kindness like, \"If you want more kindness in the\nworld, put it here.\" 说的是这块板上写满了暖心留言，并不是 Most staffers have\nreceived messages of kindness 大多数员工收到了暖心留言；选项盗用 messages of\nkindness 这一原词短语，把接受方从板偷换成员工，是张冠李戴。\n借用原文：The board has messages of kindness", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What do we learn about the man?", "options": ["A) He is a psychologist.", "B) He is a famous writer.", "C) He is a host for a TV program.", "D) He is a primary school teacher."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） He is a psychologist.\n【定位】Welcome to our program, Friends and Relationships. Our guest today is psychologist Steve\nPinker from McGill University.\n欢迎收看我们的节目《朋友与关系》。今天的嘉宾是来自麦吉尔大学的心理学家史蒂夫·\n平克。\n【信号】开场介绍嘉宾的固定句。信号词是 Our guest today is psychologist——「今天的嘉宾\n是…」是访谈节目报身份的标志句，紧跟其后的名词就是嘉宾职业。\n【替换】psychologist Steve Pinker ↔ He is a psychologist.\n同词直给——但听清职业\n【排除】B) 未提及 录音通篇没有提到男士是作家，He is a famous writer. 属于无中生有。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音里 Welcome to our program 中的 program 确实是「节目」原词，He is a host for\na TV program. 故意用 program 引诱，但说「欢迎收看节目」的是女主持人 Jane，男士是\n「嘉宾」(guest)，不是主持人。\n借用原文：Welcome to our program, Friends and Relationships.\nD) 未提及\n录音从头到尾未提及小学/教师/teach 等任何线索，He is a primary school teacher. 属于\n完全无中生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What are the speakers mainly talking about?", "options": ["A) Why social media accounts vanish without a trace.", "B) Why parents raise their children in different ways.", "C) Why people fail to respond to emails promptly.", "D) Why friends break off contact all of a sudden."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题） Why friends break off contact all of a sudden.\n【定位】Our question today is why friends suddenly disappear. They may block us on their social\nmedia accounts and stop responding to our emails. They simply vanish without a trace.\n我们今天的问题是：朋友为什么会突然消失。他们可能会在社交媒体上把我们拉黑、不再回\n我们的邮件，就这么毫无踪迹地消失了。\n【信号】Our question today is… 是节目抛题信号。信号词是 Our question today is why\nfriends suddenly disappear——访谈节目用 Our question today is…/Today we will talk\nabout… 这种句式直接点出本期话题，后面的从句就是主旨。\n【替换】why friends suddenly disappear ↔ Why friends break off contact all of a sudden.\n同义改写——disappear ↔ break off contact，suddenly ↔ all of a sudden\nblock us on their social media accounts and stop responding to our emails ↔\nfriends break off contact\n详述↔概括\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nWhy social media accounts vanish without a trace. 抠走了录音里的 social media\naccounts 和 vanish without a trace 两个原词，听到就想选；但录音说的是「朋友」会通\n过拉黑社交账号等方式消失，主体是「朋友」，不是「账号本身」——选项把「朋友消失」\n的一种具体表现错读成了主题，属以偏概全。\n借用原文：They may block us on their social media accounts\nB)\n录音确实在 The answer may lie in childhood, the way the person related to their\nmom and dad. 谈到了童年与父母关系，但那是嘉宾对「为什么朋友会突然消失」的解释；\n选项 Why parents raise their children in different ways. 把这个解释方向错抬成了主题，\n主题是「消失」不是「育儿方式」。\n借用原文：The answer may lie in childhood\nC) 原词陷阱\nWhy people fail to respond to emails promptly. 抠走了录音原词 stop responding to\nour emails，但「不回邮件」只是「朋友消失」的一种具体形态而已，把它当主题同样是以\n偏概全，且和 A 犯一样的错。\n借用原文：stop responding to our emails", "points": ["听力·篇章", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "How do children with an anxious attachment style react to a breakdown in their relationship with parents?", "options": ["A) They simply shut themselves down.", "B) They avoid showing their emotions.", "C) They scream to get their parents back.", "D) They attempt to ignore the whole situation."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They scream to get their parents back.\n【定位】When there is a relationship breakdown with their parents, children with an anxious\nattachment style scream and cry to get their parents to come back. But other children simply\nshut down and try to deal with their emotions by ignoring the situation.\n当他们和父母的关系破裂时，焦虑型依附的孩子会尖叫、哭闹，把父母叫回来；而另一些孩\n子则会直接封闭自己，试着通过忽视这种情境来处理自己的情绪。\n【信号】对照结构 anxious …. But other children …。信号词是 But other children——嘉宾用\nBut 把两类孩子的反应并排对照。这种对照句是出题铁矿，正确反应几乎都在 But 两侧之\n一。题干问的是 anxious 那一类，对应 But 前半部分。\n【替换】scream and cry to get their parents to come back ↔ They scream to get their parents back.\n选项几乎是原句裁剪\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThey simply shut themselves down. 抠走了录音原词 simply shut down，听到 simply\nshut down 就想选；但录音明确说这是 But other children(另一类，即回避型)的反应，不\n是焦虑型的反应。\n借用原文：But other children simply shut down\nB)\nThey avoid showing their emotions. 描述的是「回避情绪」，更接近回避型(But other\nchildren…try to deal with their emotions by ignoring)；焦虑型 scream and cry 反而是\n大力外露情绪，与本选项相反。\n借用原文：try to deal with their emotions by ignoring the situation\nD) 原词陷阱\nThey attempt to ignore the whole situation. 抠走的是录音原词 ignoring the\nsituation，听到就想选；但这同样是 But other children 那一类(回避型)的反应，不是焦虑\n型的反应。\n借用原文：try to deal with their emotions by ignoring the situation", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does the man say about insecurely attached people?", "options": ["A) They may regard any difference as the end of a relationship.", "B) They are on better terms with friends and romantic partners.", "C) They try to express their feelings and thoughts effectively.", "D) They attach more value to their relationships with others."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） They may regard any difference as the end of a relationship.\n【定位】However, an insecurely attached person won't. They might either attack you, get defensive, or\njust disappear from your life. To them, a difference, no matter how small in your eyes, signals\nthe end of the relationship.\n然而，不安全依附的人就不会这样。他们要么攻击你、要么变得防御、要么干脆从你生活里\n消失。在他们看来，再小的分歧，都意味着这段关系的终结。\n【信号】However + To them 双重信号锁定结论。信号词一：However, an insecurely attached\nperson won't——However 是转折信号，前面说安全型怎么处理，转折后才是题干所问的\n不安全型。信号词二：To them, a difference, no matter how small in your eyes, signals\nthe end of the relationship.——To them 是对前面群体下定论的总结句，是嘉宾真正要传\n达给观众的「金句」，几乎必出题。\n【替换】To them, a difference, no matter how small in your eyes, signals the end of the relationship. ↔\nThey may regard any difference as the end of a relationship.\n同义改写——signals ↔ regard…as，no matter how small ↔ any\n【排除】B)\nThey are on better terms with friends and romantic partners. 是正面描述「和朋友/恋\n人相处更好」，但录音里嘉宾对不安全型的描述是 They might either attack you, get\ndefensive, or just disappear from your life.，是关系处理失败的方向，选项与录音方向完\n全相反。\n借用原文：They might either attack you, get defensive, or just disappear from your life.\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey try to express their feelings and thoughts effectively. 抠走的是录音原词 express\ntheir own feelings and thoughts effectively，听到 express…effectively 就想选；但这句\n话是嘉宾对 securely attached(安全型)人的描述，不是问的不安全型。\n借用原文：They're able to hear what the other person is saying and express their own feelings and\nthoughts effectively.\nD)\nThey attach more value to their relationships with others. 说「他们更看重关系」，录\n音通篇没有这种说法。相反，录音说他们把小分歧当作关系终结的信号，是「轻易抛弃关\n系」的方向，不是「更看重关系」。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What makes the man choose to buy jeans made in Japan?", "options": ["A) Their price.", "B) Their color.", "C) Their quality.", "D) Their design."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Their quality.\n【定位】Also, the fabric they use is of a higher quality and the dyes are natural. They have a denser\nweave, and are more durable than any other jeans in the world.\n而且他们用的面料质量更高，染料是天然的。这种牛仔裤织得更密，比世界上任何其他牛仔\n裤都更耐用。\n【信号】比较级 higher / denser / more durable 集中爆发。the fabric they use is of a higher\nquality 与 more durable than any other jeans in the world 连用三处比较级——比较级\n在听力里几乎等同于『考点信号灯』，它在为日本牛仔裤的某项属性给出对比性证据。\n【替换】the fabric they use is of a higher quality ↔ Their quality.\n原文 higher quality 直接对应选项 quality\nmore durable than any other jeans in the world ↔ Their quality.\ndurable 是 quality 的下位表现\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文 They should be around $500. 提到了具体价格，让粗心的考生听到数字就奔\n『price』；但价格出现在男士交代规格那段，根本不是他选择日本货的『理由』，男士选日\n本货的理由集中在第 9 段的质量描述，Their price. 是典型原词陷阱。\n借用原文：They should be around $500.\nB) 未提及\n全文没有出现任何关于 color 的描述，Their color. 属于无中生有；它和 quality/design 一\n样是『物品属性词』，被列在这里只为凑成四项并列结构干扰考生。\nD) 原词陷阱\n原文末段 they're very fashionable too 与 But they are very special 容易让人联想到『款\n式好』而选 Their design.；但 fashionable/special 在原文里是男士补充说明价格值不值时\n的次要修饰，不是他选『日本货』的核心理由——核心理由是第 9 段集中讲的质量。\n借用原文：they're very fashionable too", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "Why is the woman puzzled by the man's request?", "options": ["A) Jeans are a typical American garment.", "B) America makes the best-known brands of jeans.", "C) America has the best weaving tools in the world.", "D) Jeans are available in a greater variety in America."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Jeans are a typical American garment.\n【定位】Really? I had no idea Japan made jeans. I would have thought America made the best jeans\nbecause it's a typical American garment.\n真的吗？我都不知道日本还做牛仔裤。我原本以为美国生产最好的牛仔裤，因为牛仔裤是典\n型的美国服饰。\n【信号】心理动词过去式 had no idea / would have thought。I had no idea Japan made\njeans 与 I would have thought America made the best jeans 紧跟 because it's a\ntypical American garment——『原本以为…因为它是典型美国服饰』这一句直接交代了女\n士困惑的根源。\n【替换】it's a typical American garment ↔ Jeans are a typical American garment.\n代词 it 还原为 jeans 后字面等同\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n原文 I would have thought America made the best jeans 与 more durable than any\nother jeans in the world 都出现了 best 类用语，容易误导考生选含 best-known 的\nAmerica makes the best-known brands of jeans.；但女士原话说的是 best jeans（最好\n的牛仔裤），不是 best-known brands（最知名的品牌），后者是偷换概念。\n借用原文：I would have thought America made the best jeans\nC) 张冠李戴\n原文 Japan still has some very old mills with the traditional weaving tools 说的是『日\n本』有传统织布工具，America has the best weaving tools in the world. 把这条属性硬\n安到 America 头上，是典型张冠李戴；同时女士困惑的依据也不是『美国有最好的织布工\n具』，而是『牛仔裤是美国典型服饰』。\n借用原文：Japan still has some very old mills with the traditional weaving tools\nD) 未提及\n原文从未做过『美国牛仔裤种类比日本多』的比较，Jeans are available in a greater\nvariety in America. 凭空造出 greater variety 这一比较，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the man say about the dyes of Japanese jeans?", "options": ["A) They are artificial.", "B) They are natural.", "C) They are unique.", "D) They are special."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They are natural.\n【定位】Also, the fabric they use is of a higher quality and the dyes are natural.\n而且他们用的面料质量更高，染料是天然的。\n【信号】dyes are natural 是关键词后直接表语。the dyes are natural——题干关键词 dyes 在原\n文里只出现一次，紧跟 are + 形容词 natural，完全是『关键词原句锁定 + 表语取答』的标\n准结构。\n【替换】the dyes are natural ↔ They are natural.\n原词复现下的指代还原\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey are artificial. 是 the dyes are natural 的直接反义，原文明确说染料是天然的，\nartificial 与之极性相反，属于反向干扰。\n借用原文：the dyes are natural\nC) 答非所问\n原文整段 9 强调日本牛仔裤独特优良（traditional weaving tools / superior / denser\nweave），容易让人选 They are unique.；但题干特定问 dyes，原文给的形容词是\nnatural，unique 是把『整段对日本牛仔裤的整体评价』错位安到 dyes 头上。\n借用原文：But the end result is surely superior.\nD) 答非所问\n原文 But they are very special. 中的 special 形容的是『日本牛仔裤这件物品』，不是\ndyes；They are special. 把整体的 special 嫁接到了局部 dyes 上，与 C 同类错法。\n借用原文：But they are very special.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the man say about Japanese jeans at the end of the conversation?", "options": ["A) They are for casual wearing.", "B) They are popular with boys.", "C) They are much too pricey.", "D) They are worth the price."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （态度观点题） They are worth the price.\n【定位】They are a little pricey, yes. But they are very special. Bear in mind, they will last me several\nyears and they're very fashionable too. I think they're totally worth it.\n是的，它们是有点贵。但它们很特别。要知道，它们能穿好几年，款式也很时髦。我觉得它\n们完全值这个价。\n【信号】让步 but + 下结论 I think…totally worth it。They are a little pricey, yes. But they are\nvery special. 是让步转折，I think they're totally worth it. 是男士在对话末段亲口下的结\n论——But 之后才是真意，I think + totally 是态度题答案句的金信号。\n【替换】I think they're totally worth it. ↔ They are worth the price.\ntotally worth it 与 worth the price 同义\nthey will last me several years ↔ They are worth the price.\n耐用是『值这个价』的下位证据\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文 Do you typically spend that much on a pair of casual trousers? 里 casual 出现在\n女士的反问中，并非男士对 Japanese jeans 的表态；They are for casual wearing. 直接搬\n走 casual 一词制造熟悉感，但题干特定问『男士说』，casual 不是男士的观点。\n借用原文：Do you typically spend that much on a pair of casual trousers?\nB) 未提及\n原文从未提到日本牛仔裤『在男生中流行』，They are popular with boys. 完全是从\n『jeans 这种衣物大众印象=年轻男孩穿的』的常识跳过来的，与录音内容无关。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文 They are a little pricey, yes. 确实承认贵，They are much too pricey. 把这句话强化\n为『贵得过分』；但男士紧跟一个 But 把态度翻到 totally worth it，C 只取了让步前半、\n丢了真意。much too pricey 的『过分嫌贵』也与男士的 a little pricey + 值得相矛盾。\n借用原文：They are a little pricey, yes.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "态度观点题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "How does the speaker feel every time he walks out of a superhero movie?", "options": ["A) He desires more in life.", "B) He wants to see it again.", "C) He feels as inspired as other audience members.", "D) He longs to become a superstar himself."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） He desires more in life.\n【定位】Every time I walk out of a superhero movie, I feel the same way. I experience a sense of\nlonging, realizing that there should be more in life.\n每次我走出超级英雄电影的影厅，感受都一样。我体验到一种渴望，意识到生活里应该有更\n多东西。\n【信号】题干与原文几乎同句开篇。Every time I walk out of a superhero movie, I feel the same\nway. 这句与题干 every time he walks out of a superhero movie 几乎逐词对应，紧跟其\n后的 I experience a sense of longing, realizing that there should be more in life. 就是\n对感受的具体说明——一句话点题、下一句给答案，是篇章题最常见的开门见山结构。\n【替换】a sense of longing ↔ desires\nlonging 与 desire 同义\nthere should be more in life ↔ more in life\n原话直接搬\n【排除】B) 未提及\nHe wants to see it again. 原文从头到尾没说“想再看一遍”，only 说每次走出影院都有同样\n的感受——这是把“every time（每次都）”误读成了“want to see again（想再看）”。\nC) 原词陷阱\nHe feels as inspired as other audience members. 盗用了原文 being inspired 这个词，\n但原文是说“hero stories 让人 inspired”是普遍道理，且根本没说“和其他观众一样被启发”\n——加了“as … as other audience members”这层比较，是凭空添料。\n借用原文：being inspired\nD)\nHe longs to become a superstar himself. 用了 longs 这个 long 的近义动词来引诱，但\n原文只说“渴望生活里有更多东西”，从未说“渴望自己也成为超级巨星”——这是把抽象的“渴\n望”过度具体化成“想当超级巨星”。\n借用原文：I experience a sense of longing", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What does the passage say about the plot of all superhero movies?", "options": ["A) It is rather unrealistic.", "B) It is extremely artistic.", "C) It is somewhat complicated.", "D) It is relatively predictable."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is relatively predictable.\n【定位】The plot of each superhero film has become relatively predictable: the world is in peril, the\nworld needs a hero, the hero is in peril, the hero resolves the peril, the hero saves the world.\n每部超级英雄电影的剧情已变得相对可预测：世界陷入危机，世界需要英雄，英雄陷入危\n机，英雄化解危机，英雄拯救世界。\n【信号】题干关键词 plot 原词复现 + 冒号定义式说明。The plot of each superhero film has\nbecome relatively predictable 里 plot 与题干 the plot of all superhero movies 是同一\n指代，冒号后面用“世界陷入危机→英雄出场→英雄解危→拯救世界”五段式具体例证\npredictable——既给定义又给例证，是答案句的典型信号。\n【替换】relatively predictable ↔ relatively predictable\n原词原义直接复现\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIt is rather unrealistic. 盗用了原文 numerous punches that land unrealistically well 里\n的 unrealistically，但原文说的是“拳头打得不真实”，是描写动作镜头，不是说整个 plot\n（剧情）不现实——把局部修饰偷换成整体判断。\n借用原文：numerous punches that land unrealistically well\nB) 张冠李戴\nIt is extremely artistic. 里 artistic 一词在原文出现的位置是第 3 段 They say the films\nlack any form of artistry.——是精英说“缺乏 artistry”，恰好与“extremely artistic”相反；\n且谈论对象是 films 整体而非 plot。\n借用原文：They say the films lack any form of artistry.\nC)\nIt is somewhat complicated. 与原文判断方向相反——第 3 段 These movies are\ncriticized as being too simple. 明说被批评为 too simple（太简单），与 complicated\n（复杂）完全对立。\n借用原文：These movies are criticized as being too simple.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What do the elites of American society think of superhero movies?", "options": ["A) They are biased against women.", "B) They are basically misleading.", "C) They are full of shootings.", "D) They are too simple."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They are too simple.\n【定位】The elites of our society have rejected superhero films. They say the films lack any form of\nartistry. These movies are criticized as being too simple.\n我们社会的精英已经拒绝了超级英雄电影。他们说这些电影毫无艺术性可言。这些电影被批\n评为太过简单。\n【信号】题干主语 elites 原词出现 + 三连否定评价。The elites of our society have rejected\nsuperhero films. 中的 elites 与题干 the elites of American society 一致，紧跟 They say\n与 are criticized 两个评价领句词，连出三句负面评价（rejected / lack any form of\nartistry / too simple），其中最后一句 too simple 就是核心答案信号。\n【替换】These movies are criticized as being too simple. ↔ They are too simple.\n被动转主动 + too simple 原词复现\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThey are biased against women. 盗用了原文 women desire to play an irreplaceable\nrole 里的 women 一词，但第 4 段是作者引用作家观点说“女性想参与伟大冒险”，跟精英的\n看法毫无关系；且原文从未说电影“对女性有偏见”。\n借用原文：women desire to play an irreplaceable role in a great adventure\nB) 过度推断\nThey are basically misleading. 用 misleading 替换了原文里的批评，但原文精英的批评只\n有“lack artistry”和“too simple”，没有任何“误导观众”的意思——属于自行升级原文负评的\n过度推断。\nC) 未提及\nThey are full of shootings. 里的 shootings（枪战）一词在原文里根本没出现，唯一接近\n的是 numerous punches（拳头），但 punches 是打斗、不是枪战，且这是讲套路不是精\n英观点。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the passage say about storytelling?", "options": ["A) It can highlight leadership.", "B) It can help connect people.", "C) It is a means to inspire creative thinking.", "D) It is an intuitive way to solidify friendship."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It can help connect people.\n【定位】Storytelling forges connections among people.\n讲故事会在人与人之间建立起紧密的联系。\n【信号】首段并列直陈句。这是录音开头一气连说的核心结论之一：Telling stories is one of the\nmost powerful means leaders have to influence, teach and inspire. 之后紧跟\nStorytelling forges connections among people.，用 forges 这个动作动词直接给\nstorytelling 下功能判断，是典型的『答案落在直陈结论句』的信号。\n【替换】forges connections among people ↔ help connect people\n动词同义＋名转动\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音首句出现 means leaders have to influence, teach and inspire，A 项把 leaders 与\nleadership 嫁接成 highlight leadership，听到 leaders 就想选 A 是典型原词陷阱；但原\n文是说『讲故事是领导者影响他人的手段』，并未说讲故事能突显领导力。\n借用原文：means leaders have to influence, teach and inspire\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音里出现 influence, teach and inspire，inspire 的宾语并不是 creative thinking；C 项\n把 inspire 抓出来后强行接上 creative thinking，构成『词对了搭配错了』的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：influence, teach and inspire\nD) 偷换概念\n原文第二段确实有 help solidify relationships，D 项把 solidify 留住、把 relationships 偷\n换成 friendship，缩小了范围。讲故事建立的是各种关系（包括职场关系），不专指友谊。\n借用原文：help solidify relationships", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What can good stories do to listeners according to the passage?", "options": ["A) Allow them to recite data points.", "B) Make them more open to learning.", "C) Enable them to remember the main idea.", "D) Stimulate them to engage in discussions."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Make them more open to learning.\n【定位】They allow the listener to enter the story, making them more open to learning.\n好故事让听者走进故事里，从而使他们更愿意去学习新东西。\n【信号】主语 They + listener 直接出现。第三段开头 Good stories do more than create a\nsense of connection. 起句后，紧跟一句 They allow the listener to enter the story,\nmaking them more open to learning。主语 They 回指 Good stories、对象 listener 直\n接出现，是题干两要素正合的句子，几乎一定是答案句。\n【替换】making them more open to learning ↔ more open to learning\n几乎原句搬运\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音里讲 more engaging than the recital of data points，意思是故事比『罗列数据』更\n吸引人——『让听众背数据』恰恰是讲故事要避免的反例。A 项把这个反向参照偷换成『允\n许他们背数据』，方向反了。\n借用原文：more engaging than the recital of data points\nC) 偷换概念\n录音说 Good stories can contain multiple meanings, so they're surprisingly\neconomical in conveying complex ideas in graspable ways.，意思是故事能以可理解的\n方式传达复杂观点，但没说『让听者记住 main idea』。C 项把 graspable ways 与\nconveying ideas 模糊归并成『记住主旨』，属于概念偷换。\n借用原文：conveying complex ideas in graspable ways\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里既出现 more engaging than the recital of data points or a discussion of\nabstract ideas，D 项就把 engage 和 discussion 拼凑成 engage in discussions，但原文\n是把『抽象观点的讨论』当成对照面，并不是说故事让听者参与讨论。\n借用原文：a discussion of abstract ideas", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What can leaders do with a simple personal story?", "options": ["A) Inspire listeners' imagination.", "B) Convey fundamental values.", "C) Enrich their own experience.", "D) Explain insightful ideas."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Convey fundamental values.\n【定位】with a simple personal story, they can convey fundamental values.\n凭借一个简单的个人故事，领导者就能传递出根本性的价值观。\n【信号】题干双锚点同句出现。第四段中 with a simple personal story, they can convey\nfundamental values 一句同时出现 simple personal story（与题干 a simple personal\nstory 完全对应）与代词 they（回指上文的 leaders），是题干两个关键词联合命中的句\n子，必然就是答案承载句。\n【替换】convey fundamental values ↔ Convey fundamental values\n原句搬运\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音末句出现 they are more likely to inspire others to want to know more，A 项把\ninspire 抓出来后接上 listeners' imagination，但原文是说激发别人『想了解更多』而非激\n发『想象力』；inspire 的宾语被偷换了。\n借用原文：inspire others to want to know more\nC) 张冠李戴\n录音里 They can offer insight into the evolution of their own experience and\nknowledge，their own experience 指的是 leaders 自己的经验，而且动作是『提供洞\n察』而非『丰富经验』；C 项把『让别人看见自己的经验』偷换成『丰富他们的经验』，主\n体与动作都偏了。\n借用原文：offer insight into the evolution of their own experience and knowledge\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里出现 offer insight into the evolution of their own experience and knowledge，\nD 项 Explain insightful ideas. 用 insightful 这个同根词钓人，但原文动作是『提供对自身\n经验演变的洞见』，不是『解释富有洞见的观点』；同根词＋搭配错位是典型陷阱。\n借用原文：offer insight into the evolution of their own experience and knowledge", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does the passage say statistics prove?", "options": ["A) Immigrants outnumber U.S.-born Americans.", "B) Immigrants have been contributing to the U.S.", "C) Another wave of immigrants is hitting the U.S.", "D) The number of immigrants to the U.S. is declining."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Immigrants have been contributing to the U.S.\n【定位】The U.S. was founded by successive waves of immigrants, and immigrants are still\ncontributing to America today—statistics prove it.\n美国是由一波又一波的移民建立的，而移民们今天仍在为美国做出贡献——统计数据证明了\n这一点。\n【信号】开头点题 + 题干关键词 statistics 原词出现。这句话出现在 P1，正是篇章的开篇点题句；\n题干里的关键词 statistics prove 在原文里以 statistics prove it 原词出现，it 指代的就是前\n半句 immigrants are still contributing to America today——这是典型的「代词回指找答\n案」信号。\n【替换】immigrants are still contributing to America today ↔\nImmigrants have been contributing to the U.S.\nare still contributing today 与 have been contributing 的时态同义改写\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 Immigrants outnumber U.S.-born Americans. 把录音里反复出现的 immigrants 和\n后文 U.S.-born individuals 拼到一起，编造出「移民人数超过本土美国人」的关系，但原\n文从未比较两群体的人数。\n借用原文：From 1996 to 2016, the pace at which immigrants started businesses was double that of\nU.S.-born individuals.\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Another wave of immigrants is hitting the U.S. 盗用了原文 successive waves of\nimmigrants 里的 wave 一词，但原文说的是「美国由一波波移民建立」，不是「现在又来\n一波」，时间和事件都被改了。\n借用原文：The U.S. was founded by successive waves of immigrants\nD) 与原文相反\n选项 The number of immigrants to the U.S. is declining. 说移民数量在下降，但原文\nimmigrants are still contributing to America today 强调的是移民在持续贡献，且后文还\n说从 1996 到 2016 移民创业速度是本土的两倍，整体方向是积极的，不存在数量下降。\n借用原文：immigrants are still contributing to America today", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What do we learn about immigrants in the U.S., compared with native-born Americans?", "options": ["A) More of them expect their children to succeed in business.", "B) They have fewer chances to be hired by U.S. companies.", "C) They have founded most Fortune 500 companies.", "D) More of them are successful business people."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） More of them are successful business people.\n【定位】From 1996 to 2016, the pace at which immigrants started businesses was double that of U.S.-\nborn individuals. Immigrants aren't just becoming entrepreneurs more frequently. They're\noften succeeding. Over 40% of Fortune 500 companies had a founder who was either an\nimmigrant or the child of one.\n从 1996 年到 2016 年，移民创业的速度是美国本土出生者的两倍。移民不仅创业更频繁，\n而且经常获得成功。超过 40% 的财富 500 强公司，其创始人要么是移民，要么是移民的\n子女。\n【信号】比较结构 double that of + 递进 aren't just…They're often succeeding。double\nthat of U.S.-born individuals 是与题干 compared with native-born Americans 直接对\n应的比较信号；紧接着的 Immigrants aren't just becoming entrepreneurs more\nfrequently. They're often succeeding. 是 not just…but also 类递进结构，递进后面的\nsucceeding 是这道题的真正落点。\n【替换】Immigrants aren't just becoming entrepreneurs more frequently. They're often succeeding. ↔\nMore of them are successful business people.\n「entrepreneurs + succeeding」概括为「successful business people」\nOver 40% of Fortune 500 companies had a founder who was either an immigrant or the child of one\n↔ More of them are successful business people.\n用 Fortune 500 创始人占比作为「成功生意人多」的具象证据\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 More of them expect their children to succeed in business. 盗用了原文 the child\nof one 里的 child，编出「期望子女在事业上成功」这一根本没说的意思。原文只是说\nFortune 500 创始人有的是移民、有的是移民子女，并未提及移民对子女的期望。\n借用原文：Over 40% of Fortune 500 companies had a founder who was either an immigrant or the\nchild of one.\nB) 与原文相反\n选项 They have fewer chances to be hired by U.S. companies. 说移民被美国公司雇佣的\n机会更少，但原文整段说的是移民自己创业、自己当 founder，根本不是「被雇佣」的角\n度，更没说机会更少。\n借用原文：the pace at which immigrants started businesses was double that of U.S.-\nborn individuals\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 They have founded most Fortune 500 companies. 盗用了录音里非常醒目的\nFortune 500，但原文是 Over 40% of Fortune 500 companies had a founder who was\neither an immigrant or the child of one，是「超过 40%」且「创始人之一是移民或移民\n子女」，绝不是 most（大多数）。这是典型的范围/程度被偷改。\n借用原文：Over 40% of Fortune 500 companies had a founder who was either an immigrant or the\nchild of one.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What does the passage say about immigrants in the U.S., in terms of financial strength?", "options": ["A) They have higher installment loan debt than native-born Americans.", "B) Nineteen percent of them borrow money from friends and family.", "C) Their level of debt is lower than that of native-born Americans.", "D) Thirty-four percent of them use credit for their daily purchases."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Their level of debt is lower than that of native-born Americans.\n【定位】Another key measure of financial strength is debt level. Immigrants do better in this area too.\nImmigrants use credit less often for purchasing cars, homes or starting businesses. This\nresults in significantly lower levels of debt than native-born Americans.\n另一项衡量经济实力的关键指标是债务水平。移民在这方面表现也更好。移民较少使用信贷\n购买汽车、住房或创业。这使得他们的债务水平显著低于美国本土出生者。\n【信号】比较结构 lower…than + 因果信号 This results in。significantly lower levels of debt\nthan native-born Americans 是与题干 compared with native-born Americans 完全对\n应的比较句；前面的 This results in 是因果信号，把「少用信贷」的原因和「债务更低」的\n结果连起来，使这句成为本段的核心结论句。\n【替换】This results in significantly lower levels of debt than native-born Americans. ↔\nTheir level of debt is lower than that of native-born Americans.\n几乎是原文的「近义同结构改写」\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n选项 They have higher installment loan debt than native-born Americans. 盗用了原文\ninstallment loan debt，但原文明确说移民的债务「显著更低」，这里却说「更高」，方向\n完全反掉。\n借用原文：This results in significantly lower levels of debt than native-born Americans.\nB) 张冠李戴\n选项 Nineteen percent of them borrow money from friends and family. 盗用了原文\njust 19% of the general population 里的 19% 和后文 they borrow money from\nfriends and family 中的借钱方式，但 19% 在原文里指的是 general population（一般人\n口）里「没有信用卡或分期贷款债务」的比例，根本不是「19% 的移民找亲友借钱」。两\n个原词被强行拼到一起，是典型的张冠李戴。\n借用原文：just 19% of the general population\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Thirty-four percent of them use credit for their daily purchases. 盗用了原文 34%\nof Americans born in Latin America 的 34%，但原文是说 34% 的拉美裔美国人「没有」\n信用卡或分期贷款债务，选项却说「34% 的人用信用卡做日常消费」，对象不对、行为也\n反过来了，还加上了 daily purchases 这个原文没有的限定。\n借用原文：34% of Americans born in Latin America report no credit card or installment loan debt.", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What do immigrants do to achieve their goals in the U.S.?", "options": ["A) Keep their traditional values and old habits.", "B) Find employment in competitive businesses.", "C) Borrow money from financial institutions.", "D) Collaborate with native-born Americans."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Keep their traditional values and old habits.\n【定位】Why are immigrants doing so well? The values and habits they bring from their own\nhomelands help explain some of the success.\n为什么移民们做得这么好？他们从故乡带来的价值观与习惯，可以解释他们部分的成功。\n【信号】设问句 Why + 解释句中的 values and habits。Why are immigrants doing so well? 是\n篇章独白里典型的设问句，紧接着的 The values and habits they bring from their own\nhomelands help explain some of the success. 是直接回答这个设问的解释句，整段功能\n就是「解释移民达成目标的原因」。这是篇章独白里最强的「答案信号」之一。\n【替换】The values and habits they bring from their own homelands ↔\nKeep their traditional values and old habits.\n「从故乡带来的价值观和习惯」=「保留传统价值观和旧习惯」\nindividuals must save more in order to achieve their goals ↔\nKeep their traditional values and old habits.\nin order to achieve their goals 与题干呼应，save more 是 old habits 的具体表现\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项 Find employment in competitive businesses. 盗用了原文反复出现的 businesses，\n但原文说的是移民自己 started businesses（自己创业），而非 find employment（找工\n作被雇佣），且原文从未提及行业「竞争激烈」。\n借用原文：the pace at which immigrants started businesses was double that of U.S.-\nborn individuals\nC) 与原文相反\n选项 Borrow money from financial institutions. 盗用了原文 they borrow money from\nfriends and family rather than a bank 里的 borrow money，但原文明确说移民是「找亲\n友借，而不是找银行」，financial institutions（金融机构）正好就是 bank 那一边，选项\n把方向反掉了。rather than 是这道题的关键否定信号。\n借用原文：they borrow money from friends and family rather than a bank\nD) 未提及\n选项 Collaborate with native-born Americans. 提出「与本土美国人合作」，但全文只出\n现「移民 vs 本土美国人」的对比，从未提及二者合作。属于无中生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affordable", "B) ancestor", "C) crucial", "D) depend", "E) initial", "F) insist", "G) just", "H) located", "I) mostly", "J) operating", "K) perhaps", "L) pioneer", "M) potential", "N) rising", "O) scheduled"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) located\n动词\n【词性槽】be 动词 is 后缺成分，构成被动 be + 过去分词\n【依据】be located in 是'位于'的固定表达\nlocated 与空后的 in the city 构成 be located in（位于某地），正好回答'电站在哪\n里'，语义和搭配双双吻合：The Noor solar power station is __(26)__ in the city\nof Ouarzazate。\n【竞争词】D) depend 搭配错，depend 配 on 不配 in\ndepend 表'依赖'要搭 depend on，且'电站依赖某城市'语义不通，与空后 in 也不搭。\nF) insist 语义不通，电站不会'坚持'\ninsist 是'坚持主张'，主语须是人或机构，'电站坚持于某城'完全讲不通。\nJ) operating 时态/语态不对，主动进行不合句意\nis operating 是'正在运转'，但下文 once completed, will generate 表明电站尚未建成，谈不\n上正在运转，逻辑冲突。\nO) scheduled 搭配错，scheduled 配 to 不配 in\nbe scheduled 后通常接 to do（预定要做某事），接 in + 地点表'位于'不成立；它真正的归宿\n是 27 空。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affordable", "B) ancestor", "C) crucial", "D) depend", "E) initial", "F) insist", "G) just", "H) located", "I) mostly", "J) operating", "K) perhaps", "L) pioneer", "M) potential", "N) rising", "O) scheduled"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) scheduled\n动词\n【词性槽】It's + 空 + to be → be + 过去分词 + 不定式\n【依据】be scheduled to do = 预定要做某事\nIt's __(27)__ to be completed soon. → It's scheduled to be completed soon，'它预定\n将很快完工'，be scheduled to do 是固定搭配，正好衔接前句 once completed, will\ngenerate（一旦建成就发电）所暗示的'尚未完工但即将完工'。\n【竞争词】D) depend 搭配错，depend 配 on 不接 to be done\ndepend 要搭 depend on，'它依赖被完成'语义和搭配都不成立。\nF) insist 主语是物，不能'坚持'\ninsist 主语须为人/机构，且无 be insisted to do 这种用法，电站不会'坚持要被完成'。\nJ) operating is operating to be 句型不成立\noperating 是现在分词，is operating to be completed 句型讲不通，逻辑上电站也尚未运转。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affordable", "B) ancestor", "C) crucial", "D) depend", "E) initial", "F) insist", "G) just", "H) located", "I) mostly", "J) operating", "K) perhaps", "L) pioneer", "M) potential", "N) rising", "O) scheduled"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) mostly\n副词\n【词性槽】comes 与 from 之间缺修饰 → 副词\n【依据】mostly = 主要地，呼应下句50%目标\nMorocco's current energy comes __(28)__ from imports. 说现状'主要靠进口'，正好与下\n句 The nation hopes to get 50 percent of its energy from renewable sources by\n2030. 形成'现在主要进口、将来转向可再生'的对照，mostly（主要地）语义最贴。\n【竞争词】G) just just 在此偏'仅仅'，与下文规模矛盾\njust 作副词常表'仅仅'，'仅仅来自进口'语气偏轻，且 just 真正的归宿是 35 空的 just how（到\n底多么）固定语境。\nK) perhaps perhaps 表推测，与陈述事实冲突\nperhaps 是'也许'，但本句在陈述当前能源结构的客观事实，不是猜测，用'也许来自进口'语气\n不对。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affordable", "B) ancestor", "C) crucial", "D) depend", "E) initial", "F) insist", "G) just", "H) located", "I) mostly", "J) operating", "K) perhaps", "L) pioneer", "M) potential", "N) rising", "O) scheduled"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) rising\n形容词\n【词性槽】with + 名词 + 空，作 demand 的状态修饰\n【依据】rising 配 at a rate of 7% 表'以7%速率增长'\nWith demand for energy __(29)__ at an annual rate of 7 percent，demand rising at a\nrate of 7 percent 即'需求以每年 7%的速率增长'，rising（上升的）与后面的'年增长率'数\n据精确呼应。\n【竞争词】A) affordable 义不搭，需求无所谓'负担得起'\naffordable 指价格可负担，修饰商品/价格，不能说'需求是负担得起的'，且与 at a rate 不搭。\nC) crucial 义不搭，且不能接 at a rate\ncrucial 是'至关重要的'，'需求至关重要地以 7%'读不通；它真正的归宿是 30 空。\nE) initial initial 表'最初的'，与'年增长率'无关\ninitial 指'起初的、最初阶段的'，无法描述一个持续的增长速率，语义与 at an annual rate\n冲突。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affordable", "B) ancestor", "C) crucial", "D) depend", "E) initial", "F) insist", "G) just", "H) located", "I) mostly", "J) operating", "K) perhaps", "L) pioneer", "M) potential", "N) rising", "O) scheduled"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) crucial\n形容词\n【词性槽】a + 空 + part → 名词前定语 → 形容词\n【依据】crucial part = 关键的一环，褒义贴目标\nthe new solar plant could be a __(30)__ part of that goal，承接前句'2030 年要 50%靠\n可再生'的宏大目标，新电站是实现该目标'至关重要的一环'，crucial（关键的）感情色彩与\n分量都吻合。\n【竞争词】E) initial initial 表'最初的'，与'实现目标的关键'错位\ninitial part 是'最初的部分/起步阶段'，但文意强调电站对达成目标的'重要性'，不是强调它是'第\n一步'，语义指向不同，分量也偏弱。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 E) —— initial 词性也对、'最初的一环'也通。a __(30)__ part of\nthat goal 填 initial 语法毫无破绽，'最初的部分'听着也合理；但全文强调新电站对达成\n50%可再生目标的'关键作用'，强调重要性而非时间先后，故选 crucial。防坑：近义形容词\n二选一时，回原文确认作者到底在强调'重要'还是'最初'。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affordable", "B) ancestor", "C) crucial", "D) depend", "E) initial", "F) insist", "G) just", "H) located", "I) mostly", "J) operating", "K) perhaps", "L) pioneer", "M) potential", "N) rising", "O) scheduled"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) pioneer\n名词\n【词性槽】a + 空（+介词短语）作表语 → 名词\n【依据】pioneer = 先锋，褒义且呼应'forerunner'\nThis makes Morocco a big __(31)__ in the field of solar energy in the Arab\nregion，'使摩洛哥成为太阳能领域的一大先锋'，pioneer（先锋/开拓者）褒义贴切，并与\n同段 It could also be a forerunner for many other countries（先行者）同义呼应，是命\n题人埋的同义线索。\n【竞争词】B) ancestor ancestor 指'祖先'，用于人/血统不用于领域\nancestor 是'祖先、先辈'，指血缘世系，不能说一个国家是某领域的'祖先'，与 in the field of\nsolar energy 搭不上。\nM) potential potential 在此句作名词指'潜力'，语义错位\na big potential 是'巨大潜力'，但本句说摩洛哥已经'成为'领域里的角色（make...a big ___），\n需要的是'角色身份'类名词而非'潜力'；potential 该用在 33 空。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affordable", "B) ancestor", "C) crucial", "D) depend", "E) initial", "F) insist", "G) just", "H) located", "I) mostly", "J) operating", "K) perhaps", "L) pioneer", "M) potential", "N) rising", "O) scheduled"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) depend\n动词\n【词性槽】关系词 that 后缺谓语 → 动词\n【依据】depend on foreign imports = 依赖外国进口\ncountries in the world that __(32)__ on foreign imports of energy，depend on（依\n赖）正配空后的 on，'依赖能源外国进口的国家'语义通顺，且呼应前文 Morocco's current\nenergy comes __(28)__ from imports. 的'靠进口'主线。\n【竞争词】F) insist insist on 义为'坚持主张'，逻辑不合\ninsist on 虽也搭 on，但义为'坚决主张做某事'，'坚持外国能源进口'不合此处'依赖进口'的语境\n逻辑。\nJ) operating 形态不对，从句需原形谓语\noperating 是分词，不能直接作 that 从句的谓语（缺助动词），且 operate on 语义也不通。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 F) —— insist on 搭配看着也成立。that __(32)__ on foreign\nimports of energy 里 insist on 与 depend on 都能搭 on，最易误选；但'坚持外国进口'不\n合'依赖进口'的文意。防坑：搭配相同就比谁的词义贴上下文逻辑，依赖（depend）才是本\n篇主线。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affordable", "B) ancestor", "C) crucial", "D) depend", "E) initial", "F) insist", "G) just", "H) located", "I) mostly", "J) operating", "K) perhaps", "L) pioneer", "M) potential", "N) rising", "O) scheduled"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) potential\n名词\n【词性槽】have huge + 空 → 形容词后名词中心词\n【依据】huge potential for = 巨大潜力，搭配天成\nthe Middle East and North Africa have huge __(33)__ for solar energy projects，have\nhuge potential for（在……方面有巨大潜力）是地道搭配，'中东北非太阳能项目潜力巨\n大'语义通顺，正承接下句讲的'阳光充足、技术更便宜'两大有利条件。\n【竞争词】B) ancestor ancestor 指'祖先'，与'地区有潜力'毫不相干\nancestor 是血缘'祖先'，'地区有巨大祖先'完全讲不通，且无 have ancestor for 用法。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affordable", "B) ancestor", "C) crucial", "D) depend", "E) initial", "F) insist", "G) just", "H) located", "I) mostly", "J) operating", "K) perhaps", "L) pioneer", "M) potential", "N) rising", "O) scheduled"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) affordable\n形容词\n【词性槽】become more 后接空 → 形容词比较级中心词\n【依据】技术变得更'便宜'才会被推广\n本段讲该地区适合太阳能的原因，partly because of adequate sunlight and partly\nbecause technology has become more __(34)__ in the region。技术'变得更负担得起\n（成本更低）'正是推动太阳能项目的合理原因，affordable 语义最贴；这也与下段 how\ncompetitive solar technologies can be（太阳能技术多有竞争力）相呼应。\n【竞争词】C) crucial crucial 不能比较成'更关键'修饰技术成本\n'技术变得更至关重要'读着别扭，且本句因果是'技术更便宜→项目更可行'，crucial 不切此因\n果；它该用在 30 空。\nE) initial initial 表'最初的'，无比较级语义\ninitial 指'最初阶段的'，本身不表程度，说'技术变得更最初的'逻辑不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affordable", "B) ancestor", "C) crucial", "D) depend", "E) initial", "F) insist", "G) just", "H) located", "I) mostly", "J) operating", "K) perhaps", "L) pioneer", "M) potential", "N) rising", "O) scheduled"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) just\n副词\n【词性槽】空 + how + 形容词 → 修饰整个感叹/疑问短语\n【依据】just how... = '到底多么……'，加强语气\na realization of __(35)__ how competitive solar technologies can be → just how\ncompetitive，just 在 how 前作'究竟、到底'解，强调'人们意识到太阳能技术到底有多大竞\n争力'，语气贴合专家感叹的口吻。\n【竞争词】K) perhaps perhaps 表猜测，与'已意识到'矛盾\nperhaps 是'也许'，但句子说 a realization of...（已经意识到了），是确定的认知而非猜测，'也\n许多么有竞争力'语气自相矛盾，且 perhaps 不与 how 连用作此加强义。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Chase Carey believes greater use should be made of digital technology to make Formula One more accessible to its fans.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】数字技术让球迷更易接触\n【定位】Increased digital access for fans, a more behind-the-scenes experience for broadcast viewers\nand innovation in areas like virtual reality—what is it like to speed around a track inside a\nFerrari?—are among the possibilities.\n增加球迷的数字接入、为转播观众提供更多幕后体验、以及虚拟现实等领域的创新——坐在\n法拉利里绕赛道飞驰是什么感觉？——都是可能的方向。\n【改写】greater use should be made of digital technology ↔ innovation in areas like virtual reality\n上位词对具体手段\nmore accessible to its fans ↔ make the fans connect to the live experience as much as possible\n“更易接触”=“让球迷尽量连接”\n【辨邻】 F F 段说 alter the way fans experience the sport，方向相近，但未提 digital\ntechnology，承载“数字化”的是 G 段。\nalter the way fans experience the sport, both in person and remotely", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Chase Carey was deeply impressed by the ceremony preceding last year's Monaco race.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】去年摩纳哥赛前仪式\n【定位】He recalled attending Formula One's Monaco race last year and being overwhelmed by\nthe ceremony leading up to the event, the way the race charmed the city for days ahead of\nthe start.\n他回忆起去年参加 F1 摩纳哥站，被开赛前的仪式深深震撼，那场比赛在开赛前数日就让整\n座城市为之着迷。\n【改写】deeply impressed by the ceremony ↔ being overwhelmed by the ceremony\n“深受触动”=“被震撼”\npreceding last year's Monaco race ↔ leading up to the event\n“在……之前”=“通向……”\n【辨邻】 M M 段讲 on race day 赛车冲出隧道的场面，与题干 ceremony preceding 的赛前仪式\n不是同一节点。\nThen, on race day, he watched as the cars rocketed out of a tunnel", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "One of Chase Carey's goals is to make Formula One more appealing to Americans.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】目标：对美国人更有吸引力\n【定位】Among the goals, Carey said in an interview on Tuesday, is one that just about every global\nsport seems interested in chasing: increasing interest in the United States.\nCarey 在周二的采访中说，目标之一是几乎所有全球性运动都想追求的：提升在美国的关\n注度。\n【改写】make Formula One more appealing to Americans ↔ increasing interest in the United States\n“更有吸引力”=“提升兴趣”\nOne of Chase Carey's goals ↔ Among the goals, Carey said\n“目标之一”同形\n【辨邻】 I I 段说 very little crossover，谈两类受众差异，并未陈述“目标是吸引美国人”。\nthere was very little crossover", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "A former motorbike parts dealer led Formula One for the past forty years.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】摩托配件商领导四十年\n【定位】For the past four decades, the leader of Formula One car racing, one of the biggest annual\nsporting series in the world, was Bernie Ecclestone, a former motorcycle parts dealer who\nbuilt it into an international presence essentially on his own.\n过去四十年间，世界最大年度赛事系列之一 F1 的领导者是 Bernie Ecclestone，一个摩托\n车配件商出身、几乎凭一己之力把它做成国际赛事的人。\n【改写】A former motorbike parts dealer ↔ a former motorcycle parts dealer\n近乎原词复现\nfor the past forty years ↔ For the past four decades\n四十年=四个十年\n【辨邻】 B B 段说他自认 a dictator，刻画统治风格，并未陈述题干的职业出身与年限。\nthe sport was at its best when he was allowed to act as \"a dictator\"", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Chase Carey thought the audience of Formula One could be made to share his feeling about the race.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】观众能共享他对比赛的感受\n【定位】He was fascinated. \"You can't help but be awed,\" he said, \"and I think that feeling can be\ntranslated to the viewer.\"\n他被深深吸引。“你不可能不为之惊叹，”他说，“而且我认为这种感受能够传递给观众。”\n【改写】the audience of Formula One could be made to share his feeling ↔\nthat feeling can be translated to the viewer\n“让观众共享”=“感受传给观众”\n【辨邻】 L L 段说他 being overwhelmed by the ceremony，止于个人感受；题干核心 share\nhis feeling 的“分享给观众”只在 M 段。\nbeing overwhelmed by the ceremony leading up to the event", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Chase Carey used to serve as a top advisor for a major broadcasting company.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】曾任大广播公司高级顾问\n【定位】At Fox Broadcasting Company, he was a top advisor for years, known for his skill in helping\nto lead the launch of the company into sports, as well as the start of Fox News Channel.\n在福克斯广播公司，他多年担任高级顾问，以助推公司进军体育、并参与创办福克斯新闻频\n道的本事著称。\n【改写】serve as a top advisor ↔ he was a top advisor\n职衔原样复现\na major broadcasting company ↔ Fox Broadcasting Company\n上位泛称对专名\n【辨邻】 C C 段说他是 a former executive with Fox Broadcasting Company 并被任命接替，\n重点是接任；题干的 top advisor 履历细节在 J 段。\na former executive with Fox Broadcasting Company and DirecTV", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Chase Carey intends to make connections easier between the audience and the Formula One racers.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】让观众与车手更易建立联系\n【定位】and second, alter the way fans experience the sport, both in person and remotely, so that\nconnections between the audience and people within the series are easier to make.\n其二，改变球迷体验这项运动的方式，无论现场还是远程，从而让观众与赛事内部人员之间\n更容易建立联系。\n【改写】make connections easier between the audience and the Formula One racers ↔\nconnections between the audience and people within the series are easier to make\n“车手”属“赛事内部人员”\n【辨邻】 G G 段说目标是 make the fans connect to the live experience，对象是“现场体验”；\n题干 between the audience and the Formula One racers 强调观众与人之间的联\n系，在 F 段。\nmake the fans connect to the live experience as much as possible", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "The new leader of Formula One admitted he was not super interested in car racing.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】新帅承认自己不太爱赛车\n【定位】Chase Carey—a former executive with Fox Broadcasting Company and DirecTV who by his\nown admission is not a fierce racing fan—was named to replace Ecclestone and to try to\nrenovate the organization's management, reach and ambition.\nChase Carey——一位曾任福克斯广播公司和 DirecTV 高管、自认并非狂热赛车迷的人\n——被任命接替 Ecclestone，并试图革新该组织的管理、影响力与抱负。\n【改写】admitted he was not super interested in car racing ↔\nby his own admission is not a fierce racing fan\n“承认不太感兴趣”=“自认非狂热车迷”\nThe new leader of Formula One ↔ was named to replace Ecclestone\n“新领导”=“接替前任”\n【辨邻】 B B 段写 Ecclestone 自认当 a dictator 时最好，对象是旧领导；题干 The new leader\n指 Carey，在 C 段。\nthe sport was at its best when he was allowed to act as \"a dictator\"", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "People's opinions differ as to whether Formula One can be promoted in the U.S.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】能否在美国推广意见不一\n【定位】The larger question, though, is a familiar one: Is there room for Formula One in the ever-\ncrowded sports landscape of the United States? Opinions vary, particularly because viewing\nhabits among consumers continue to evolve.\n然而更大的问题是个老问题：在日益拥挤的美国体育版图里，F1 还有容身之地吗？意见不\n一，尤其因为消费者的观看习惯仍在演变。\n【改写】People's opinions differ ↔ Opinions vary\n“意见不同”=“意见不一”\nwhether Formula One can be promoted in the U.S. ↔\nIs there room for Formula One in the ever-crowded sports landscape of the United States?\n“能否推广”=“是否有容身之地”\n【辨邻】 K K 段引人说 I think they can build Formula One in the U.S.，是肯定立场；题干\nopinions differ 强调分歧，由 H 段提出。\nI think they can build Formula One in the U.S.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Compared with other racing series, Formula One focuses more on speed and involves more danger.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】比别的赛事更重速度、更危险\n【定位】It's about speed, danger and risk. And Formula One has that more than any other\nracing series.\n重点在于速度、危险与风险。而在这一点上，F1 比任何其他赛车系列都更突出。\n【改写】Compared with other racing series, Formula One focuses more on speed ↔\nAnd Formula One has that more than any other racing series\n“比其他更”比较对齐\ninvolves more danger ↔ speed, danger and risk\n“危险”原词复现\n【辨邻】 I I 段说 the Nascar fan base is a very different fan base，比的是粉丝；题干\nspeed、danger 的比较在 K 段。\nOther than they're both cars, the Nascar fan base is a very different fan base", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What problem have supermarkets been facing?", "options": ["A) They are actually on the way to bankruptcy.", "B) They have been losing customers and profits.", "C) They are forced to use e-commerce strategies.", "D) They have difficulty adapting to climate change."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What problem have supermarkets been facing? 直接问一个具体事实——超市在面\n临什么难处，答案应能在原文找到明确对应句。\n【拆句】What problem have supermarkets been facing? 是一个 what 引导的特殊疑问句，主干\n是\"超市一直在面对什么难处\"，have been facing 是现在完成进行时，强调\"长期、持续\"在\n面对，这个时态暗示原文会有 long/for more than a decade 一类的长期信号词，结构本\n身无陷阱。\n【定位】Supermarkets have long been suffering as one of the thinnest-margined businesses in\nexistence and one of the least-looked-forward-to places to work or visit. For more than a\ndecade, they have been under attack from e-commerce giants, blamed for making Americans\nfat, and accused of contributing to climate change.\n长期以来，超市一直是利润最薄的行业之一，也是人们最不愿去工作或光顾的场所之一。十\n多年来，它们一直受到电商巨头的攻击，被指责让美国人发胖，还被指控加剧气候变化。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\n原文只说超市利润薄、处境难，从未说它\"正走向破产\"；on the way to bankruptcy\n把\"利润薄\"夸大成\"濒临破产\"，程度严重超出原文。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthinnest-margined businesses ↔ losing customers and profits\nunder attack from e-commerce giants ↔ losing customers\n预判落在\"生意难做、客流利润受损\"，四个选项里只有 They have been losing\ncustomers and profits. 同时覆盖了原文的\"利润薄\"和\"受电商攻击\"，与预判最契合。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文说超市\"受电商攻击\"，并没说它们\"被迫去采用电商策略\"；forced to use e-\ncommerce strategies 把\"被攻击\"偷换成\"被迫效仿\"，是原文未提的内容。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文是超市\"被指责加剧气候变化\"（超市是被指责方），而 have difficulty adapting to\nclimate change 说成\"超市难以适应气候变化\"，把\"被指责制造问题\"换成了\"自己适应\n不了\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the passage say about the idea of a self-service grocery?", "options": ["A) It was put forward by King Kullen.", "B) It originated in the United States.", "C) It has been under constant debate.", "D) It proves revolutionary even today."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the passage say about the idea of a self-service grocery? 问的是原文\n就这个想法\"说了什么\"，是对原文具体陈述的核对，属细节题。\n【拆句】What does the passage say about the idea of a self-service grocery? 主干是\"原文关于\n这个想法说了什么\"，of the idea of a self-service grocery 是层层嵌套的 of 介词短语作后\n置定语，只是在限定\"哪个想法\"，剥掉修饰后主干很清楚，无理解陷阱。\n【定位】The revolutionary idea of a self-service grocery, where people could hunt and gather food\nfrom aisles rather than asking a clerk to fetch items from behind a counter, first came about\nin America.\n让人们能在货架间自行寻找采集食物、而不必请店员从柜台后取货的自助式杂货这一革命性\n想法，最早出现在美国。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\n原文说金库伦是公认的\"第一家超市\"，并未说这个\"想法\"是金库伦\"提出\"的；put\nforward by King Kullen 把\"第一家店\"错安成\"想法的提出者\"。\nB) ✓ 正确\nfirst came about in America ↔ It originated in the United States.\n预判核心是\"起源地是美国\"，四项里只有 It originated in the United States. 准确对应\n定位句的起源信息，故锁定。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文 some debate about which was the very first 争论的是\"哪家是第一家店\"，而\n非\"这个想法本身\"；under constant debate 把争论对象由\"谁是第一家\"偷换成\"这个\n想法\"。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文只说该想法当年是 revolutionary，并称超市 may not feel cutting-edge now\n（如今未必前沿），并无\"至今仍具革命性\"之说；proves revolutionary even today 是\n原文没有的论断。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What did supermarkets do by adopting the Fordist factory approach?", "options": ["A) They modernized traditional groceries in many ways.", "B) They introduced cutting-edge layout of their stores.", "C) They improved the quality of the food they sold.", "D) They revolutionized the distribution of goods."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What did supermarkets do by adopting the Fordist factory approach? 问超市靠\n这一做法\"做成了什么\"，是对原文一个具体结果的核对，属细节题。\n【拆句】What did supermarkets do by adopting the Fordist factory approach? 主干是\"超市做\n了什么\"，by adopting... 是 by + 动名词的方式状语，表示\"通过采用福特式工厂做法这一\n手段\"，by 在此不是\"被\"而是\"通过\"，基础薄弱者易误读，点明即可。\n【定位】The supermarket imitated the Fordist factory, with its emphasis on efficiency and\nstandardization, and reimagined it as a place to buy food. Supermarkets may not feel cutting-\nedge now, but they were a revolution in distribution at the time.\n超市模仿了讲求效率与标准化的福特式工厂，并把它重新设想成一个买食物的地方。超市如\n今也许感觉不再前沿，但它们在当时是分销领域的一场革命。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文只说超市模仿福特工厂、成为分销革命，没说它\"在许多方面把传统杂货店现代\n化\"；modernized traditional groceries in many ways 是原文未提的笼统说法。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文紧接着说超市 may not feel cutting-edge now（如今未必前沿），且布局九十年\n几乎没变，并非\"引入了前沿布局\"；cutting-edge layout 与原文意思相反。\nC) 无中生有\n原文谈的是分销方式的革命，全程未涉及\"食品质量\"的提升；improved the quality of\nthe food 在原文找不到任何依据。\nD) ✓ 正确\na revolution in distribution ↔ They revolutionized the distribution of goods.\n预判核心是\"在分销上掀起革命\"，四项里只有 They revolutionized the distribution of\ngoods. 与定位句 a revolution in distribution 直接等值，故锁定。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What is the typical supermarket layout intended to do?", "options": ["A) Arouse customers' appetite to buy flowers, fruit and vegetables.", "B) Provide customers easy access to items they want to buy.", "C) Induce customers to make more unplanned purchases.", "D) Enable customers to have a more enjoyable shopping experience."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is the typical supermarket layout intended to do? 问布局的具体意图，是对\n原文一处目的陈述的核对，属细节题。\n【拆句】What is the typical supermarket layout intended to do? 主干是\"布局意在做什么\"，be\nintended to do 是\"被设计用来做……\"的固定结构，是被动语态，主语 layout 是被设计的\n对象，看懂这个被动是解题前提，结构上无其他陷阱。\n【定位】Meanwhile, they keep the milk, eggs, and other daily basics all the way back so you'll travel\nthrough as much of the store as possible, and be tempted along the way.\n与此同时，超市把牛奶、鸡蛋等日用必需品全放在最里面，好让你穿过尽可能多的店面，一\n路上被诱惑。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\n原文说前端摆鲜花果蔬只是 a breath of freshness to arouse our appetite（唤起食\n欲）的一个手段，最终目的是让人走遍全店；appetite to buy flowers, fruit and\nvegetables 把局部手段当成了整体布局的意图。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文布局恰恰把必需品放到最里、逼顾客绕远，并非\"方便顾客买想买的东西\"；easy\naccess to items they want to buy 与原文意图相反。\nC) ✓ 正确\ntravel through as much of the store as possible, and be tempted along the way ↔\nInduce customers to make more unplanned purchases.\n预判核心是\"让顾客多走多买计划外的东西\"，四项里只有 Induce customers to make\nmore unplanned purchases. 抓住了布局的真实意图，故锁定。\nD) 无中生有\n原文讲的是诱导顾客多买，从未说布局是为\"让购物更愉快\"；more enjoyable\nshopping experience 是原文没有的说法。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What have people long feared about supermarkets?", "options": ["A) They use tricky strategies to promote their business.", "B) They are going to replace the local groceries entirely.", "C) They apply corporate black magic to the goods on display.", "D) They take advantage of the weaknesses of women shoppers."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What have people long feared about supermarkets? 问人们长期害怕的具体内\n容，是对原文一处陈述的核对，属细节题。\n【拆句】What have people long feared about supermarkets? 主干是\"人们长期害怕超市什么\"，\nhave long feared 是现在完成时夹一个副词 long，强调\"长久以来一直害怕\"，about\nsupermarkets 是 fear 的对象，结构清楚无陷阱。\n【定位】We have long feared that this \"revolution in distribution\" uses corporate black magic on\nour appetite.\n长期以来我们一直害怕，这场\"分销革命\"在用企业的黑魔法操纵我们的食欲。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nuses corporate black magic on our appetite ↔\nThey use tricky strategies to promote their business.\n预判核心是\"用隐秘手段操纵食欲\"，四项里只有 They use tricky strategies to\npromote their business. 把\"黑魔法\"这一比喻还原为\"狡猾手段\"，与定位句契合，故\n锁定。\nB) 无中生有\n原文讲的是人们怕超市操纵食欲，全程未提\"取代本地杂货店\"；replace the local\ngroceries entirely 是原文没有的内容。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文是黑魔法用在 our appetite（我们的食欲）上，而 black magic to the goods on\ndisplay 把作用对象偷换成\"陈列的商品\"，对象张冠李戴。\nD) 以偏概全\n关于女性的内容出自被引用的《隐形说客》一书、只是个例证，并非人们\"长期\"害怕的\n核心；weaknesses of women shoppers 拿书中一个局部例子当成了普遍的担忧。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "Why did America's traditional school year have a three-month summer vacation?", "options": ["A) Students needed to help with farm work.", "B) Students needed time to learn necessary farming skills.", "C) The agricultural society then attached less importance to academics.", "D) America lagged behind other countries in making a scientific school calendar."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】Why did America's traditional school year have a three-month summer vacation? 问\n一个写在文中的具体事实原因，答案应能在原文直接找到。\n【拆句】Q51 是一个 Why 引导的特殊疑问句，主干就是 school year have a three-month\nsummer vacation，没有从句也没有否定，结构平铺直叙、无陷阱。\n【定位】The traditional school year, with three months of vacation every summer, was first\nimplemented when America was an agricultural society and the summer months were needed\nfor farm work.\n传统学年带有每年夏季三个月假期，它最初设立于美国还是农业社会、夏季几个月需要用于\n农活的时候。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthe summer months were needed for farm work ↔ Students needed to help with farm work\n预判的\"夏天要干农活\"与 Students needed to help with farm work 完全吻合，故锁定\n此项。\nB) 无中生有\n原文只说夏季需要去做农活，从未提学生需要时间去\"学习\"农业技能，learn necessary\nfarming skills 文中没有依据。\nC) 无中生有\n原文只说当时是农业社会、夏天要务农，并未说农业社会 attached less importance to\nacademics，这是文中没有的因果。\nD) 张冠李戴\nAmerica lagged behind other countries 出自第二段 With the U.S. lagging behind\nother countries in academics，那是\"如今\"现状，并非当初设三个月暑假的原因，张冠\n李戴。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What benefit will year-round schooling bring students in addition to improving their learning?", "options": ["A) It will help them get back to where their lessons started.", "B) It will enable them to absorb what they have learned.", "C) It will familiarize them with the school environment.", "D) It will strengthen their relationship with teachers."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What benefit will year-round schooling bring students in addition to improving their\nlearning? 问一项写在文中的具体好处，属可直接核对的事实。\n【拆句】Q52 主干是 What benefit will year-round schooling bring students，后接介词短语\nin addition to improving their learning 作限定，提醒答案要排除学业类好处，是理解\n关键。\n【定位】But year-round schooling isn't just about academics. Teachers and students experience a\ncloser relationship in year-round schools than they do in traditional schools and, in the\nabsence of any long-term break, students do not feel detached from the school environment.\n但全年制不仅仅关乎学业。在全年制学校里，师生关系比在传统学校里更亲近，而且由于没\n有任何长期假期，学生不会感到与校园环境疏离。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nget back to where their lessons started 套用第二段 get back to where they were，\n那讲的是暑期遗忘后补回进度，属学业范畴，被题干限定排除，且原文是消除这一损耗\n而非\"帮学生回到起点\"。\nB) 答非所问\nabsorb what they have learned 仍是讲学习吸收，属 improving their learning 范\n畴，被题干\"除学业外\"排除。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文说的是学生 do not feel detached from the school environment（不疏离），并\n非 familiarize them with the school environment（使其熟悉环境），把\"不疏远\"偷\n换成\"变熟悉\"。\nD) ✓ 正确\nTeachers and students experience a closer relationship ↔\nstrengthen their relationship with teachers\n预判的\"师生关系更近\"正对应 strengthen their relationship with teachers，且它落\n在\"学业之外\"的范围内，故选此项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What do some childhood development experts believe about the long summer vacation?", "options": ["A) It meets students' need to study on their own.", "B) It enables students to learn about the outside world.", "C) It satisfies students' desire to stay longer at home.", "D) It contributes to students' healthy growth."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What do some childhood development experts believe about the long summer\nvacation? 问的是专家在文中直接表述的观点，可逐字核对，不需要读者推断。\n【拆句】Q53 主干 What do some childhood development experts believe，后接 about the\nlong summer vacation 点明评论对象；注意主语是 experts 而非作者，是\"这话谁说的\"的\n关键。\n【定位】Some childhood development experts believe that time off from school is vital to healthy\ndevelopment as kids are not designed to spend so much of their time inside classrooms and\nthe summer break provides a perfect opportunity to get outside.\n一些儿童发展专家认为，离开学校的休息时间对健康成长至关重要，因为孩子天生不适合把\n这么多时间花在教室里，而暑假正好提供了走到户外的绝佳机会。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n专家强调的是休息和户外，从未说放假满足学生 study on their own（自学）的需要，\n原文无此意。\nB) 过度推断\n原文只说暑假给孩子 a perfect opportunity to get outside（到户外的机会），并不\n等于 learn about the outside world（了解外部世界），后者把\"到户外\"拔高成\"认识\n世界\"。\nC) 张冠李戴\n专家观点里没有\"满足学生 stay longer at home\"的说法；待在家看电视是后文作者反驳\n时提到的现实，并非专家所赞同的需求。\nD) ✓ 正确\ntime off from school is vital to healthy development ↔\nIt contributes to students' healthy growth\n预判的\"放假利于健康成长\"正对应 It contributes to students' healthy growth，故选\n此项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What is the argument against the experts' idea of a long summer vacation?", "options": ["A) It does little good to most students.", "B) It benefits few students playing outside.", "C) It leads students to neglect their studies.", "D) It makes students addicted to computer games."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What is the argument against the experts' idea of a long summer vacation? 问一个写\n在文中的具体反驳理由，可直接核对。\n【拆句】Q54 主干 What is the argument against the experts' idea，介词 against 决定方向\n是\"反对专家\"；读懂 against 才不会把专家自己的话误当反驳，是理解关键。\n【定位】The problem with this argument is that most children aren't playing outside or even spending\ntime with other kids. While some children visit summer camps, most stay at home, watching\nTV or playing games on electronic devices, which hardly benefits them.\n这个论点的问题在于，大多数孩子并没有在户外玩耍，甚至没有和别的孩子相处。虽然有些\n孩子去参加夏令营，但多数人待在家里看电视或玩电子设备上的游戏，这对他们几乎没有\n好处。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nmost stay at home, watching TV or playing games on electronic devices, which hardly benefits\nthem\n↔ It does little good to most students\n预判的\"对多数孩子没好处\"正对应 It does little good to most students，故选此项。\nB) 以偏概全\n原文 hardly benefits them 说的是待在家的\"多数\"孩子没好处，benefits few students\nplaying outside 却把对象缩成\"在外面玩的少数\"，偷改了范围。\nC) 无中生有\n作者反驳讲的是放假对孩子无益，并未说放假让学生 neglect their studies（荒废学\n业），文中无此意。\nD) 以偏概全\n原文把看电视和玩电子设备游戏并列为现象之一，并未断言放假使学生 addicted to\ncomputer games（沉迷电脑游戏），把众多居家活动之一夸成\"上瘾\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-2-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What does the author think of the traditional school year in the U.S. today?", "options": ["A) Well-grounded.", "B) Culture-bound.", "C) Outdated.", "D) Welcomed."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （态度题）\n【判型】What does the author think of the traditional school year in the U.S. today? 问的是作\n者对旧学制的整体评价，需通览全文立场而非某句。\n【拆句】Q55 主干 What does the author think of the traditional school year，关键是 today\n——问的是\"在今天看来\"旧学制如何，提示要结合文章\"时代已变\"的论调判断，是解题\n关键。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nWell-grounded（有充分根据的）是褒义，意味旧学制合理；而作者全篇主张它该改，\n态度恰好相反。\nB) 无中生有\n文章把旧学制归因于\"农业经济\"这一时代背景，并未从\"文化\"角度评价，Culture-\nbound（受文化局限）在原文找不到落点。\nC) ✓ 正确\nThe U.S. has changed from a farming economy to a knowledge- and innovation-based economy,\nso it makes sense for the school year to change as well\n↔ Outdated\n预判的\"旧学制已不合时宜、该改\"与 Outdated 一词对应，故选此项。\nD) 与原文相反\nWelcomed（受欢迎的）是褒义，暗示作者赞许旧学制；而作者主张它该改、显然不持\n欢迎态度，方向相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "态度题"]}]});
